    "slug": "how-to-check-dog-food-recalls",
    "title": "How to Check Dog Food Recalls: FDA, Lot Codes, and What to Do Tonight",
    "description": "How to check dog food recalls: FDA pages, lot codes on bags, brand alerts, and the exact steps to take if your dog already ate a recalled lot.",
    "publishedAt": "2026-02-28T08:00:00.000Z",
    "html": "<p>Recalls are how the system admits a batch went wrong. They are not proof that a brand is evil, and the absence of a headline is not proof a bag is perfect. Checking recalls is a five-minute habit: lot code, FDA list, brand site. This Petshiwü guide is the checklist, not a panic siren.</p>\n<figure>\n  <picture><source srcset=\"/blog/kibble-bowl.webp\" type=\"image/webp\" />\n  <img src=\"/blog/kibble-bowl.jpg\" alt=\"Dog food bag turned to show a lot code and best-by date for a recall-check walkthrough\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"eager\" /></picture>\n  <figcaption>Dog food bag turned to show a lot code and best-by date for a recall-check walkthrough</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h2>Where official information lives</h2>\n<p>Start with the FDA’s animal-food recall and withdrawal pages. Brand sites and retailer notices should match those lot ranges. Social screenshots without a lot code are rumors. If a friend forwards a blurry list from 2019, check the date before you throw out a current bag.</p>\n<p>Save a photo of the bag’s lot code, best-by date, and UPC the day you open it. That photo is what you will need at 10 p.m. when a notice drops.</p>\n<h2>What a lot code looks like</h2>\n<p>Lot or batch codes are usually ink-jetted on the back or bottom seal. They are not the same as the UPC. Some brands also print a plant code. If you cannot find it, photograph the entire back panel and ask the manufacturer’s customer line the next morning.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Write the code on a tape strip on the food bin when you decant kibble.</li>\n<li>Keep the empty bag until the bin is finished.</li>\n<li>Check treats and toppers too — recalls are not only kibble.</li>\n<li>If you buy fresh food, keep the pack sleeve until the box is eaten.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>If the lot matches a recall</h2>\n<p>Stop feeding that lot. Bag it, keep the receipt, and follow the brand’s return or disposal instructions. Call your veterinarian if your dog has vomiting, diarrhea, refusal to eat, or neurologic signs — especially after a Salmonella or aflatoxin-type notice. Do not induce vomiting unless a veterinarian tells you to.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/blog/kibble-bowl.webp\" alt=\"Dog food bag turned to show a lot code and best-by date for a recall-check walkthrough\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<h2>How to think about brand risk without superstition</h2>\n<p>One recall handled transparently can be better than a quiet company. Repeated pathogen problems are a different story. Feed-grade and human-grade brands have both issued recalls. Completeness and handling still matter more than a slogan. See <a href=\"/learning/human-grade-dog-food-worth-it\">human-grade explained</a>.</p>\n<h2>Everyday prevention that is not a recall search</h2>\n<p>Store food sealed and dry. Do not feed food that smells rancid, looks moldy, or arrived with a torn bag. Report problems to the FDA and the brand. Shop replacements in <a href=\"/dog\">our dog aisle</a> only after you know which SKU is safe.</p>\n<h2>Shop this at Petshiwü</h2>\n<p>Petshiwü is a nationwide online pet store. If a product you bought is recalled, keep the bag and contact us plus your veterinarian. Free shipping over $49, no autoship. NYC delivery now; nationwide shipping soon.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/dog\">Browse matching products at Petshiwü</a> — free shipping over $49, no autoship.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>How often should I check for dog food recalls?</h3>\n<p>Check when you open a new bag and whenever a headline mentions your brand. A monthly FDA glance is plenty for most households.</p>\n<h3>What if I already threw the bag away?</h3>\n<p>You may still have a receipt, order email, or photo. Contact the retailer and brand with the product name and purchase date. Watch your dog and call the vet if signs appear.</p>\n<h3>Are homemade diets safer from recalls?</h3>\n<p>They avoid commercial lots and introduce recipe and pathogen risks of their own. See our homemade safety guide. There is no zero-risk bowl.</p>\n<h2>Related Petshiwü guides</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/homemade-dog-food-safety\">Homemade Dog Food Safety: Why Turkey-and-Rice Is Not a Diet</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/best-fresh-dog-food-2026\">Best Fresh Dog Food 2026: How to Compare Gently Cooked Meals</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/how-much-to-feed-a-dog\">How Much to Feed a Dog: Calories, Body Condition, and the Lying Cup</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>This article is educational and is not a diagnosis or treatment plan. Ask your veterinarian before you change food, supplements, or medicine, and seek care right away for blood in stool or vomit, repeated vomiting, trouble breathing, collapse, or a pet that cannot keep water down.</em></p>",
