    "slug": "best-dog-treats-2026",
    "title": "Best Dog Treats 2026: Training Bits, Chews, and the 10% Rule",
    "description": "Best dog treats 2026: how to pick training treats, safe chews, single-ingredient options, and how to keep extras under 10% of daily calories.",
    "publishedAt": "2026-03-14T14:00:00.000Z",
    "html": "<p>Treats are a training tool and a relationship. They are also how lean dogs quietly become round. In 2026 the treat aisle is louder than ever — freeze-dried, “human grade,” dental, stuffed. This Petshiwü guide ranks jobs, not vibes: tiny training bits, safer chews, and the calorie math that keeps dinner honest.</p>\n<figure>\n  <picture><source srcset=\"/blog/dog-toys.webp\" type=\"image/webp\" />\n  <img src=\"/blog/dog-toys.jpg\" alt=\"Assorted training treats and a single-ingredient chew arranged by calorie size for a 2026 treat guide\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"eager\" /></picture>\n  <figcaption>Assorted training treats and a single-ingredient chew arranged by calorie size for a 2026 treat guide</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h2>Name the job before you name the brand</h2>\n<p>Training needs pea-sized, smelly, soft bits you can fire quickly. Occupying a crate needs a longer-lasting chew that is still safe. Dental needs a product actually designed for teeth — see <a href=\"/learning/best-dental-dog-food-and-chews\">dental chews</a>. Allergy trials need treats that match the diet chemistry. One bag cannot do all four jobs well.</p>\n<p>Single-ingredient freeze-dried meats are easy to break and easy to overfeed because they are calorie-dense. Count them.</p>\n<h2>The 10% rule is a ceiling, not a target</h2>\n<p>Keep treats at or under about 10% of daily calories unless your veterinarian set a different plan. Subtract them from the meal. If you used 80 training bits in class, dinner shrinks. See <a href=\"/learning/how-much-to-feed-a-dog\">how much to feed</a>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Skip xylitol, raisins, macadamia, and “sugar-free” people candy.</li>\n<li>Read every jerky label for onion or garlic powder.</li>\n<li>Size chews longer than the muzzle and supervise.</li>\n<li>Power chewers need tougher toys, not necessarily harder bones — see <a href=\"/learning/best-toys-for-aggressive-chewers\">aggressive chewers</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Chew hardness versus tooth enamel</h2>\n<p>If you cannot dent the chew with a fingernail, think twice for a dog with a fracture history. Antlers and nylon bricks break teeth. Rubber toys and stuffed lick mats are often kinder. Match the product to the dog in front of you, not the toughest dog on the internet.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/blog/dog-toys.webp\" alt=\"Assorted training treats and a single-ingredient chew arranged by calorie size for a 2026 treat guide\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<h2>Training class logistics</h2>\n<p>Carry treats in a pouch, not a crumby pocket. Soft treats that do not crumble in heat travel better. If you use a clicker, the treat still has to land fast — see <a href=\"/learning/clicker-training-for-dogs\">clicker training</a>.</p>\n<h2>Shopping a treat drawer that will not explode</h2>\n<p>Pick one training treat, one chew, and one “jackpot.” Browse the treat aisle in <a href=\"/dog\">dog</a> and keep lot codes like you would for food. We will not invent a 2026 clinical ranking of jerky brands.</p>\n<h2>Shop this at Petshiwü</h2>\n<p>Shop training treats and chews at Petshiwü with free shipping over $49 and no autoship. NYC delivery is available now; nationwide shipping opens soon.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/dog\">Browse matching products at Petshiwü</a> — free shipping over $49, no autoship.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>What treats are best for training in 2026?</h3>\n<p>Soft, pea-sized, high-aroma bits your dog will work for. Low-calorie options help if you train a lot. Avoid hard biscuits you have to break mid-session.</p>\n<h3>Are homemade treats safer?</h3>\n<p>They can be if you control ingredients. They still count as calories and can include toxic foods by accident. Store them like human leftovers.</p>\n<h3>Can treats cause pancreatitis?</h3>\n<p>Fatty chews and table scraps can trigger susceptible dogs. Choose leaner training bits and ask your veterinarian if your dog has a history of pancreatitis.</p>\n<h2>Related Petshiwü guides</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/best-dental-dog-food-and-chews\">Best Dental Dog Food and Chews: VOHC Seals Versus Pretty Breath Mints</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/best-toys-for-aggressive-chewers\">Best Toys for Aggressive Chewers: Tough Does Not Mean Unbreakable</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/how-much-to-feed-a-dog\">How Much to Feed a Dog: Calories, Body Condition, and the Lying Cup</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>This article is educational and is not a diagnosis or treatment plan. Ask your veterinarian before you change food, supplements, or medicine, and seek care right away for blood in stool or vomit, repeated vomiting, trouble breathing, collapse, or a pet that cannot keep water down.</em></p>",
