    "title": "Best Cat Treats 2026: Training Bits, Lickables, and the Tiny Calorie Bomb",
    "description": "Best cat treats 2026: how to pick lickable tubes, freeze-dried bits, and dental treats without blowing the daily calorie budget or a food trial.",
    "publishedAt": "2026-04-12T12:00:00.000Z",
    "html": "<p>Cat treats are tiny and dense. A few lickable tubes can be a meal. In 2026 the aisle is full of mousse sticks, crunchy pillows, and freeze-dried fish. This Petshiwü guide assigns jobs: training, medication wrapping, and dental — then keeps extras under about 10% of daily calories.</p>\n<figure>\n  <picture><source srcset=\"/blog/kitten.webp\" type=\"image/webp\" />\n  <img src=\"/blog/kitten.jpg\" alt=\"Lickable cat treat tube and freeze-dried bits arranged by calorie size for a 2026 treat guide\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"eager\" /></picture>\n  <figcaption>Lickable cat treat tube and freeze-dried bits arranged by calorie size for a 2026 treat guide</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h2>Name the job</h2>\n<p>Lickable tubes help pill time and lazy bonding. Freeze-dried single-ingredient bits help clicker-style training. Crunchy treats are optional and easy to overfeed. Dental treats only help if the cat actually chews them. Allergy trials need treats that match the diet.</p>\n<p>Read for onion, garlic, and xylitol. People “sugar-free” snacks are not cat snacks.</p>\n<h2>Calorie math for a 10-pound cat</h2>\n<p>If dinner is 200 kcal, treats should stay near 20 kcal unless your veterinarian set a different plan. One tube can be 10–30 kcal. Subtract from the can. See <a href=\"/learning/how-much-to-feed-a-cat\">how much to feed a cat</a>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Use pea-sized pieces for training.</li>\n<li>Do not leave a pouch out to spoil.</li>\n<li>Skip tuna-in-oil as a daily treat.</li>\n<li>If the cat is on a urinary or renal diet, ask before adding grocery fish.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Picky cats and treat monsters</h2>\n<p>If dinner is ignored until the tube appears, you trained a strike. Use treats after a real meal or as a measured topper on complete food. See <a href=\"/learning/picky-cat-not-eating\">picky cat not eating</a>.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/blog/kitten.webp\" alt=\"Lickable cat treat tube and freeze-dried bits arranged by calorie size for a 2026 treat guide\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<h2>Dental and hairball treat claims</h2>\n<p>Crunchy dental snacks are a maybe. Brushing wins. Hairball treats are fiber plus flavor. Neither replaces veterinary dentistry or a comb.</p>\n<h2>Shopping a two-treat drawer</h2>\n<p>Pick one training bit and one lickable. Browse <a href=\"/cat\">the cat aisle</a>. Keep lot codes like food.</p>\n<h2>Shop this at Petshiwü</h2>\n<p>Petshiwü can send the bowls, foods, and extras this best cat treats article points to. Free shipping over $49 and no autoship. NYC delivery is available now; nationwide shipping opens soon.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/cat\">Browse matching products at Petshiwü</a> — free shipping over $49, no autoship.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>Are lickable cat treats complete food?</h3>\n<p>Most are complementary. They are not dinner. Check the label. A few brands sell complete mousse meals — those are different products.</p>\n<h3>Can kittens have treats?</h3>\n<p>Tiny amounts of complete-food-compatible treats are fine. Milk is not a treat plan. Keep calories in the growth budget.</p>\n<h3>What treats are safest for a food trial?</h3>\n<p>Only what your veterinarian approved. Many trials fail because of flavored treats and toothpaste.</p>\n<h2>Related Petshiwü guides</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/how-much-to-feed-a-cat\">How Much to Feed a Cat: Kcal, Body Condition, and the Bottomless Bowl</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/picky-cat-not-eating\">Picky Cat Not Eating: Nausea, Texture, and the 24-Hour Rule</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/best-wet-cat-food-2026\">Best Wet Cat Food 2026: Moisture, Complete Pâtés, and Picky Cans</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>This article is educational and is not a diagnosis or treatment plan. Ask your veterinarian before you change food, supplements, or medicine, and seek care right away for blood in stool or vomit, repeated vomiting, trouble breathing, collapse, or a pet that cannot keep water down.</em></p>",
