    "title": "Picky Cat Not Eating: Nausea, Texture, and the 24-Hour Rule",
    "description": "Picky cat not eating: when to call a vet, how to warm food, why rotating flavors backfires, and how hepatic lipidosis risk changes the timeline.",
    "publishedAt": "2026-04-14T13:00:00.000Z",
    "html": "<p>Cats that skip meals get sick faster than dogs. An overweight cat that stops eating can slide toward hepatic lipidosis in days. “Picky” is allowed after a veterinarian says the mouth, gut, and labs look okay. This Petshiwü page is the triage and the bowl strategy — not a flavor parade.</p>\n<figure>\n  <picture><source srcset=\"/blog/wet-cat-food.webp\" type=\"image/webp\" />\n  <img src=\"/blog/wet-cat-food.jpg\" alt=\"Cat sniffing and turning away from a bowl of wet food while a thermometer checks warmth\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"eager\" /></picture>\n  <figcaption>Cat sniffing and turning away from a bowl of wet food while a thermometer checks warmth</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h2>The 24-hour rule is shorter for some cats</h2>\n<p>A kitten, a senior, a diabetic, or a cat that already looks dull should not wait a full day. Any cat with vomiting, hiding, or a painful belly needs the clinic now. Do not syringe random milk. Do not wait for a new brand to arrive overnight.</p>\n<p>Dental pain, constipation, kidney disease, pancreatitis, and nausea from vaccines or meds all look like snobbery.</p>\n<h2>If the exam is clear, stop the buffet</h2>\n<p>Offer a measured complete meal. Warm wet food to just above room temperature. Pick it up after 20–30 minutes. Do not follow with roast chicken at 9 p.m. See <a href=\"/learning/best-wet-cat-food-2026\">wet food textures</a>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Try pâté if shreds fail, or the reverse — one change at a time.</li>\n<li>Use a wide, shallow dish so whiskers are not crammed.</li>\n<li>Sit nearby without looming; some cats eat after you leave.</li>\n<li>Ask the clinic about appetite stimulants if medical nausea is still suspected.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Food aversion is real</h2>\n<p>Cats that were sick in the hospital may refuse the prescription food they smelled while nauseated. The veterinarian can switch textures or brands. Do not punish. Do not starve a “trial” at home.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/blog/wet-cat-food.webp\" alt=\"Cat sniffing and turning away from a bowl of wet food while a thermometer checks warmth\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<h2>Toppers without wrecking a complete diet</h2>\n<p>A teaspoon of a complete mousse or a veterinarian-approved topper can help. If toppers become the meal, switch to a complete wet food and stop pretending the ignored kibble counts. See <a href=\"/learning/how-much-to-feed-a-cat\">feeding math</a>.</p>\n<h2>Shopping after the all-clear</h2>\n<p>Buy one complete food the cat already liked when healthy. Browse <a href=\"/cat\">the cat aisle</a>. Call back if the cat still will not eat.</p>\n<h2>Shop this at Petshiwü</h2>\n<p>Ready to restock after reading about picky cat not eating? Petshiwü ships as a nationwide online pet store with free shipping over $49 and no autoship. NYC delivery is available now; nationwide shipping opens soon.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/cat\">Browse matching products at Petshiwü</a> — free shipping over $49, no autoship.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>Why is my cat suddenly picky?</h3>\n<p>Pain, nausea, stress, and learned waiting for better food are common. Sudden change plus other signs is medical until proven otherwise.</p>\n<h3>Can I force-feed at home?</h3>\n<p>Do not invent a syringe plan. You can cause aspiration or food aversion. Ask the clinic for a technique or a feeding tube if needed.</p>\n<h3>Is tuna a good appetite fix?</h3>\n<p>A tiny amount of water-packed tuna is sometimes used short term under veterinary guidance. Daily tuna is a thiamine and mercury problem and trains a tuna-only cat.</p>\n<h2>Related Petshiwü guides</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/best-wet-cat-food-2026\">Best Wet Cat Food 2026: Moisture, Complete Pâtés, and Picky Cans</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/best-senior-cat-food\">Best Senior Cat Food: Protein, Phosphorus, and the 11-Year Check</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/how-much-to-feed-a-cat\">How Much to Feed a Cat: Kcal, Body Condition, and the Bottomless Bowl</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>This article is educational and is not a diagnosis or treatment plan. Ask your veterinarian before you change food, supplements, or medicine, and seek care right away for blood in stool or vomit, repeated vomiting, trouble breathing, collapse, or a pet that cannot keep water down.</em></p>",
