    "description": "Best cat litter 2026: clumping clay vs silica vs plant, dust and tracking, how many boxes you need, and when a litter change causes accidents.",
    "publishedAt": "2026-04-19T08:00:00.000Z",
    "html": "<p>Litter is the product your cat votes on every day. In 2026 the aisle is clay, silica crystals, tofu, pine, and mixed blends. The best litter is the one your cat uses, that you will scoop, with dust you can live with. This Petshiwü guide is the buyer’s filter, not a brand coronation.</p>\n<figure>\n  <picture><source srcset=\"/blog/cat-litter.webp\" type=\"image/webp\" />\n  <img src=\"/blog/cat-litter.jpg\" alt=\"Three litter types in open pans showing clump, crystal, and plant pellets for a 2026 litter comparison\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"eager\" /></picture>\n  <figcaption>Three litter types in open pans showing clump, crystal, and plant pellets for a 2026 litter comparison</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h2>Clumping clay is still the control group</h2>\n<p>Sodium bentonite clay forms scoopable clumps so you can remove urine daily and keep the box livable. It is heavy and can be dusty. If someone in the house has asthma, look at low-dust formulas or switch materials. Scented clay is a common reason cats start peeing on the bathmat.</p>\n<p>Silica crystals absorb urine and control odor with less scooping of liquid, but some cats dislike the texture. Plant litters (tofu, corn, wood) vary in clump strength and can mold if you leave wet clumps in a humid bathroom.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Plan one box per cat plus one extra.</li>\n<li>Scoop clay at least once daily.</li>\n<li>Change the type gradually — mix old and new over a week.</li>\n<li>If accidents start after a litter swap, go back to the old type and call the vet if straining is involved.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Tracking and floor reality</h2>\n<p>Fine granules track. Larger pellets track less and may not clump as well. A mat and a high-sided box help. Covered boxes trap odor and can feel like a trap to a nervous cat. See <a href=\"/learning/self-cleaning-litter-box-guide\">self-cleaning boxes</a> if scooping is the failure point.</p>\n<h2>Medical clues in the box</h2>\n<p>Tiny clumps, blood, or crying in the box is a veterinary visit — see <a href=\"/learning/cat-uti-symptoms\">UTI symptoms</a>. Do not “try a new litter” while a male cat strains.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/blog/cat-litter.webp\" alt=\"Three litter types in open pans showing clump, crystal, and plant pellets for a 2026 litter comparison\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<h2>Kittens and eaten litter</h2>\n<p>Some kittens eat clumping litter. Use a non-clumping or clinic-approved option until they stop grazing. See <a href=\"/learning/litter-box-training-a-kitten\">litter training</a>.</p>\n<h2>Shopping a litter you will actually buy again</h2>\n<p>Buy the smallest bag of a new type first. Browse litter in <a href=\"/cat\">the cat aisle</a>. Keep a backup of the known-good litter during any trial.</p>\n<h2>Shop this at Petshiwü</h2>\n<p>When you are ready to put this best cat litter article into the cart, Petshiwü is a nationwide online pet store with free shipping over $49 and no autoship. NYC delivery is available now; nationwide shipping opens soon.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/cat\">Browse matching products at Petshiwü</a> — free shipping over $49, no autoship.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>How often should I dump the whole box?</h3>\n<p>With daily scooping, many clay boxes get a full change every 2–4 weeks, sooner if odor wins. Follow the litter type’s instructions and your nose.</p>\n<h3>Is unscented better?</h3>\n<p>Usually. Cats have stronger noses than marketing departments. If you need scent, scent the room, not the box.</p>\n<h3>Do I need expensive litter for multiple cats?</h3>\n<p>You need enough boxes and daily scooping more than a luxury granule. A cheap litter you scoop beats a premium litter you ignore.</p>\n<h2>Related Petshiwü guides</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/self-cleaning-litter-box-guide\">Self-Cleaning Litter Box Guide: Rakes, Sensors, and Cats That Hate Motors</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/cat-uti-symptoms\">Cat UTI Symptoms: Straining, Tiny Clumps, and the Male-Cat Emergency</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/litter-box-training-a-kitten\">Litter Box Training a Kitten: Location, Sides, and the One-Litter Rule</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>This article is educational and is not a diagnosis or treatment plan. Ask your veterinarian before you change food, supplements, or medicine, and seek care right away for blood in stool or vomit, repeated vomiting, trouble breathing, collapse, or a pet that cannot keep water down.</em></p>",
