    "slug": "how-to-brush-dog-teeth",
    "title": "How to Brush Dog Teeth: A Two-Week Training Plan That Sticks",
    "description": "How to brush dog teeth: dog-safe toothpaste, finger brushes, a two-week desensitization plan, and when brushing is not enough without a veterinary cleaning.",
    "publishedAt": "2026-04-24T10:00:00.000Z",
    "html": "<p>Brushing is the only home habit that reliably reaches the gumline. Chews help. Water additives might help. Nothing replaces a soft brush and a dog toothpaste that does not contain xylitol. This Petshiwü plan is two weeks of training, not a wrestling match on night one.</p>\n<figure>\n  <picture><source srcset=\"/blog/dog-dental.webp\" type=\"image/webp\" />\n  <img src=\"/blog/dog-dental.jpg\" alt=\"Person brushing a dog’s canine teeth with a pet toothbrush and enzymatic toothpaste\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"eager\" /></picture>\n  <figcaption>Person brushing a dog’s canine teeth with a pet toothbrush and enzymatic toothpaste</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h2>Buy the right paste before you touch a fang</h2>\n<p>Human toothpaste is not for dogs. Xylitol is toxic. Fluoride pastes are for people. Get an enzymatic dog toothpaste and a soft finger brush or a small-headed pet brush. Let the dog lick paste off your finger for three days so the flavor is a jackpot, not a surprise.</p>\n<p>If the mouth smells like infection, the face is swollen, or the dog will not let you near the muzzle, stop and book dentistry. Brushing an abscess is cruelty.</p>\n<h2>The two-week ladder</h2>\n<p>Days 1–3: paste on a finger, lips only, treat after. Days 4–7: finger on the big canine teeth for two seconds. Days 8–10: brush those four teeth. Days 11–14: add a few premolars. You do not need a perfect 30-second set on day one. Daily short sessions beat a weekly battle.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Lift the lip; you do not need to pry the mouth open like a crocodile.</li>\n<li>Focus on the outside surfaces — that is where plaque likes to sit.</li>\n<li>Stop if you see fresh blood that is more than a pink smear; call the vet.</li>\n<li>Count dental chews as calories — see <a href=\"/learning/best-dental-dog-food-and-chews\">dental chews</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Puppies and seniors</h2>\n<p>Puppies learn faster. Make it a game before adult tartar arrives. Seniors with loose teeth need a veterinary look before you scrub. Anesthesia-free “cleanings” that only polish what you can see are not a substitute for a true dental with radiographs.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/blog/dog-dental.webp\" alt=\"Person brushing a dog’s canine teeth with a pet toothbrush and enzymatic toothpaste\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<h2>When brushing is not enough</h2>\n<p>Established tartar and pocketing need a professional cleaning. Home care keeps the next year kinder. Pair brushing with a food the dog can actually chew — see <a href=\"/learning/best-senior-dog-food-2026\">senior textures</a>.</p>\n<h2>Shopping a kit you will use</h2>\n<p>Toothpaste, a brush, and a treat pouch. Browse dental care in <a href=\"/dog\">the dog aisle</a>. We will not sell you a miracle wipe as a brush replacement.</p>\n<h2>Shop this at Petshiwü</h2>\n<p>Shop dog supplies at Petshiwü. Free shipping over $49, no autoship. NYC delivery now; nationwide shipping soon.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/dog\">Browse matching products at Petshiwü</a> — free shipping over $49, no autoship.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>How often should I brush my dog’s teeth?</h3>\n<p>Daily is the goal. Three times a week is better than never. Consistency matters more than a perfect two-minute routine.</p>\n<h3>What if my dog hates the brush?</h3>\n<p>Stay on the finger-brush step longer. Try a different paste flavor. Do not pin the dog down. Ask a trainer or vet tech for a demo.</p>\n<h3>Can I use baking soda?</h3>\n<p>Skip kitchen chemistry. Use a veterinary toothpaste designed for dogs. Baking soda tastes bad and is easy to overdo.</p>\n<h2>Related Petshiwü guides</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/best-dental-dog-food-and-chews\">Best Dental Dog Food and Chews: VOHC Seals Versus Pretty Breath Mints</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/cat-dental-care-at-home\">Cat Dental Care at Home: Finger Brushes, Gels, and the Cat Who Will Not Open</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/best-dog-treats-2026\">Best Dog Treats 2026: Training Bits, Chews, and the 10% Rule</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>This article is educational and is not a diagnosis or treatment plan. Ask your veterinarian before you change food, supplements, or medicine, and seek care right away for blood in stool or vomit, repeated vomiting, trouble breathing, collapse, or a pet that cannot keep water down.</em></p>",
