    "slug": "dog-ear-infection-signs",
    "title": "Dog Ear Infection Signs: Smell, Head Shakes, and When to Stop Cleaning",
    "description": "Dog ear infection signs: odor, discharge colors, head tilting, and why cotton swabs make things worse. Educational only — infections need a veterinarian.",
    "publishedAt": "2026-04-29T12:00:00.000Z",
    "html": "<p>Ear infections announce themselves with smell, shake, and a dog that slaps a paw at the flap. They do not announce which yeast or bacterium is growing. This Petshiwü guide is how to recognize the problem and what not to pour in the canal. Diagnosis and medication belong to your veterinarian.</p>\n<figure>\n  <picture><source srcset=\"/blog/dog-grooming.webp\" type=\"image/webp\" />\n  <img src=\"/blog/dog-grooming.jpg\" alt=\"Person examining a dog’s ear flap without inserting a swab, showing safe at-home inspection\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"eager\" /></picture>\n  <figcaption>Person examining a dog’s ear flap without inserting a swab, showing safe at-home inspection</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h2>What you can see and smell at home</h2>\n<p>A healthy ear canal is pale pink and barely scented. Brown gunk, yellow pus, a sweet or yeasty smell, redness, and heat are reasons to book an appointment. Head tilting, circling, or loss of balance can mean the middle ear is involved — that is urgent, not a weekend cleaner project.</p>\n<p>Floppy ears, swimming, and allergies are common setups. If itch is seasonal, read <a href=\"/learning/seasonal-allergies-in-dogs\">seasonal allergies</a> after the current infection is treated.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Do not jam cotton swabs down the canal.</li>\n<li>Do not use hydrogen peroxide or alcohol as a home brew.</li>\n<li>Do not share another dog’s leftover ear drops.</li>\n<li>Stop swimming until the veterinarian clears it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Cleaning is maintenance, not treatment</h2>\n<p>A vet-approved cleaner can help after the canal is diagnosed as safe to flush. Cleaning an infected, ruptured, or foreign-body ear can push debris deeper. If the dog screams when you lift the flap, put the bottle down.</p>\n<h2>Allergies and food are the sequel</h2>\n<p>Recurrent ear infections often sit on allergic skin disease. Treating the yeast once and ignoring fleas or food trials is how you buy drops three times a year. See <a href=\"/learning/best-dog-food-for-itchy-skin\">itchy skin</a> and keep <a href=\"/learning/best-flea-and-tick-prevention-for-dogs-2026\">flea prevention</a> current.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/blog/dog-grooming.webp\" alt=\"Person examining a dog’s ear flap without inserting a swab, showing safe at-home inspection\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<h2>Hematoma and the shake cycle</h2>\n<p>Violent shaking can burst a blood vessel in the flap (aural hematoma). That needs a veterinary plan. An e-collar can protect the repair. The underlying itch still has to be found.</p>\n<h2>What to bring to the appointment</h2>\n<p>A photo of the discharge, a list of swims, and any cleaner you already used. Shop gentle cleaners only after the exam, in <a href=\"/dog\">the dog aisle</a>.</p>\n<h2>Shop this at Petshiwü</h2>\n<p>Petshiwü can send the bowls, foods, and extras this dog ear infection signs article points to. Free shipping over $49 and no autoship. NYC delivery is available now; nationwide shipping opens soon.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/dog\">Browse matching products at Petshiwü</a> — free shipping over $49, no autoship.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>Why does my dog’s ear smell like corn chips?</h3>\n<p>A mild odor can be normal yeast on the skin. A strong smell plus redness or pain is an infection until a veterinarian says otherwise.</p>\n<h3>Can I use apple cider vinegar in dog ears?</h3>\n<p>No. Home acids can burn inflamed skin and delay real treatment.</p>\n<h3>Are upright-eared dogs safe from infections?</h3>\n<p>They get fewer moisture traps, not immunity. Allergies and mites still happen.</p>\n<h2>Related Petshiwü guides</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/how-often-to-bathe-a-dog\">How Often to Bathe a Dog: Coat Type, Skin Barrier, and the Dish-Soap Mistake</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/seasonal-allergies-in-dogs\">Seasonal Allergies in Dogs: Pollen, Paws, and the Calendar Itch</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/best-flea-and-tick-prevention-for-dogs-2026\">Best Flea and Tick Prevention for Dogs 2026: Oral vs Topical vs Collar</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>This article is educational and is not a diagnosis or treatment plan. Ask your veterinarian before you change food, supplements, or medicine, and seek care right away for blood in stool or vomit, repeated vomiting, trouble breathing, collapse, or a pet that cannot keep water down.</em></p>",
