    "title": "Heartworm Prevention for Dogs: Year-Round Chews and the Test First Rule",
    "description": "Heartworm prevention for dogs: why mosquitoes still matter inland, why you test before restarting a chew, and what prevention does not treat. Vet-first guide.",
    "publishedAt": "2026-05-08T09:00:00.000Z",
    "html": "<p>Heartworm disease is a worm in the heart and lungs that starts with a mosquito bite. Prevention is a monthly (or longer-acting) medication your veterinarian prescribes. Treatment after infection is harder and riskier than the chew you skipped. This Petshiwü explainer is why we do not treat heartworm like a summer-only beach product.</p>\n<figure>\n  <picture><source srcset=\"/blog/supplements.webp\" type=\"image/webp\" />\n  <img src=\"/blog/supplements.jpg\" alt=\"Monthly heartworm preventive chew next to a calendar reminder and a mosquito-season map note\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"eager\" /></picture>\n  <figcaption>Monthly heartworm preventive chew next to a calendar reminder and a mosquito-season map note</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h2>Prevention is not a vaccine and not optional in most of the U.S.</h2>\n<p>Mosquitoes live in cities, courtyards, and inland suburbs. A dog that “never goes to the lake” still needs a plan your veterinarian sets. Most preventives also cover some intestinal parasites — read the label you were given, not a generic blog list.</p>\n<p>If you missed months, do not just restart a leftover box. Dogs can become heartworm-positive and then suffer a dangerous reaction if a preventive kills circulating microfilariae the wrong way. Test first.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Give the chew on a repeating calendar date.</li>\n<li>Watch the dog for a few hours after a new product.</li>\n<li>Tell the clinic if the dog vomits the chew.</li>\n<li>Ask about travel — see <a href=\"/learning/traveling-with-a-dog\">traveling with a dog</a> — because risk maps change.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What prevention does not do</h2>\n<p>It does not treat an adult heartworm infection by itself. It does not replace flea control. It does not mean you skip the annual test. Coughing, fainting, and a swollen belly are reasons to go in now, not to buy a different chew online.</p>\n<h2>Cats and ferrets are a different conversation</h2>\n<p>This article is for dogs. Cats can get heartworm too, with fewer treatment options. Ask the clinic about your other pets. Do not share a dog chew with a cat.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/blog/supplements.webp\" alt=\"Monthly heartworm preventive chew next to a calendar reminder and a mosquito-season map note\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<h2>Cost versus treatment</h2>\n<p>A year of prevention is usually cheaper than adulticide treatment, rest, and complications. Pet insurance may or may not cover heartworm — see <a href=\"/learning/is-pet-insurance-worth-it-2026\">insurance 2026</a>. Skipping preventives to “save” money is a bad loan.</p>\n<h2>Shopping the reminder, not a mystery import</h2>\n<p>Use the product your veterinarian prescribed. Browse related wellness items in <a href=\"/dog\">the dog aisle</a>. Be wary of unlabeled imports.</p>\n<h2>Shop this at Petshiwü</h2>\n<p>Petshiwü stocks everyday supplies that pair with heartworm prevention for dogs, without an autoship contract. Free shipping over $49. NYC delivery is available now, and nationwide shipping opens soon — start in our dog collection.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/dog\">Browse matching products at Petshiwü</a> — free shipping over $49, no autoship.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>Do indoor dogs need heartworm prevention?</h3>\n<p>In most regions, yes. Mosquitoes enter homes. Your veterinarian knows the local risk.</p>\n<h3>Can I buy heartworm chews without a test?</h3>\n<p>Many clinics require a negative test at intervals, especially if doses were missed. That is safety, not upselling.</p>\n<h3>What if I saw a mosquito in December?</h3>\n<p>That is why many vets recommend year-round prevention. Ask about your ZIP code, not a national average.</p>\n<h2>Related Petshiwü guides</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/best-flea-and-tick-prevention-for-dogs-2026\">Best Flea and Tick Prevention for Dogs 2026: Oral vs Topical vs Collar</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/is-pet-insurance-worth-it-2026\">Is Pet Insurance Worth It in 2026? Deductibles, Waiting Periods, and What Is Excluded</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/traveling-with-a-dog\">Traveling with a Dog: Cars, Hotels, and a Packing List That Includes the Vet</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>This article is educational and is not a diagnosis or treatment plan. Ask your veterinarian before you change food, supplements, or medicine, and seek care right away for blood in stool or vomit, repeated vomiting, trouble breathing, collapse, or a pet that cannot keep water down.</em></p>",
