    "slug": "fireworks-anxiety-in-dogs",
    "title": "Fireworks Anxiety in Dogs: A July Plan That Starts Before Dusk",
    "description": "Fireworks anxiety in dogs: how to build a sound-safe room, what not to give from your medicine cabinet, and why Fourth of July training starts in June.",
    "publishedAt": "2026-05-13T11:00:00.000Z",
    "html": "<p>Fireworks are unpredictable, bass-heavy, and often illegal in the alley anyway. Dogs do not understand a holiday. This Petshiwü plan is environmental management, a June sound-desensitization start, and a hard no on leftover human sedatives. Pair it with <a href=\"/learning/fourth-of-july-dog-safety\">Fourth of July safety</a> for escape risks.</p>\n<figure>\n  <picture><source srcset=\"/blog/dog-anxiety.webp\" type=\"image/webp\" />\n  <img src=\"/blog/dog-anxiety.jpg\" alt=\"Dog resting in a padded interior room with white noise and drawn blinds during a fireworks-prep setup\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"eager\" /></picture>\n  <figcaption>Dog resting in a padded interior room with white noise and drawn blinds during a fireworks-prep setup</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h2>Build the room before the first boom</h2>\n<p>Pick an interior room. Close curtains. Run a fan or a playlist so the sound floor rises before the show. Take the dog out early to toilet. Do not wait until 9 p.m. to “tire them out” in a park that is already popping.</p>\n<p>A pressure wrap helps some dogs and overheats others. Try it on a quiet night first. Stuff a <a href=\"/learning/lick-mat-benefits-for-dogs\">lick mat</a> so the mouth has a job.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Update ID tags and microchip info in June.</li>\n<li>Do not tie a dog in a yard during fireworks.</li>\n<li>Skip the rooftop party with the dog.</li>\n<li>If the dog is already panting and pacing in the afternoon, call the vet about event medication — do not wait for nightfall.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Training is a June project</h2>\n<p>Play low-volume firework recordings with food, then stop while the dog is still fine. Raise volume over weeks, not over one afternoon. If the dog shuts down, you went too fast. This is slower than a social video.</p>\n<h2>Drugs and supplements</h2>\n<p>Event medication from your veterinarian can be appropriate. Diphenhydramine makes some dogs groggy and few dogs truly calm. CBD is not a fireworks cure — see <a href=\"/learning/cbd-for-dogs-what-to-know\">CBD</a>. Never give your own Xanax, Ambien, or edible.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/blog/dog-anxiety.webp\" alt=\"Dog resting in a padded interior room with white noise and drawn blinds during a fireworks-prep setup\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<h2>After the holiday</h2>\n<p>Check fences and gates. A missing dog on July 5 is common. Keep the safe room available for New Year’s. If noise fear is year-round, ask about a behavior workup, not just a once-a-year wrap.</p>\n<h2>Shopping a calm kit</h2>\n<p>White-noise machine, lick mats, a well-fitting wrap if it helps, and a secure leash. Browse <a href=\"/dog\">the dog aisle</a>.</p>\n<h2>Shop this at Petshiwü</h2>\n<p>Use our dog collection to continue from fireworks anxiety in dogs with brands you can repurchase. Petshiwü is a nationwide online pet store: free shipping over $49, no autoship, NYC delivery now, nationwide shipping soon.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/dog\">Browse matching products at Petshiwü</a> — free shipping over $49, no autoship.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>Should I take my dog to the fireworks show?</h3>\n<p>No. Leave them in a prepared quiet room. Shows are for people.</p>\n<h3>Do thunder shirts work?</h3>\n<p>Some dogs settle; some do not. It is management, not a personality change. Fit it correctly and watch for overheating.</p>\n<h3>Can I crate a terrified dog during fireworks?</h3>\n<p>Only if the crate is already a rest place. A panicked dog in a crate can break teeth. See our crate and anxiety guides.</p>\n<h2>Related Petshiwü guides</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/fourth-of-july-dog-safety\">Fourth of July Dog Safety: Escapes, Burns, and a Quiet Room Plan</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/dog-separation-anxiety-help\">Dog Separation Anxiety Help: Panic Versus Boredom and a Real Exit Plan</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/cbd-for-dogs-what-to-know\">CBD for Dogs: What to Know About Legality, Quality, and False Cures</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>This article is educational and is not a diagnosis or treatment plan. Ask your veterinarian before you change food, supplements, or medicine, and seek care right away for blood in stool or vomit, repeated vomiting, trouble breathing, collapse, or a pet that cannot keep water down.</em></p>",
