    "title": "How to Help an Overweight Dog: Walks, Portions, and Joints That Cannot Wait",
    "description": "How to help an overweight dog: body-condition scoring, treat audits, safer exercise, and when weight gain is a thyroid or medication issue. Call your vet first.",
    "publishedAt": "2026-05-23T08:00:00.000Z",
    "html": "<p>Helping an overweight dog is mostly honest calories and kinder movement. It is also a medical screen. Hypothyroidism, steroids, and arthritis change the plan. This Petshiwü article is the household playbook that sits next to <a href=\"/learning/best-dog-food-for-weight-loss\">weight-loss food</a> — not a 30-day challenge with a fake before-and-after.</p>\n<figure>\n  <picture><source srcset=\"/blog/dog-walk.webp\" type=\"image/webp\" />\n  <img src=\"/blog/dog-walk.jpg\" alt=\"Overweight dog on a short leash walk with a handler holding a measured treat pouch\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"eager\" /></picture>\n  <figcaption>Overweight dog on a short leash walk with a handler holding a measured treat pouch</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h2>Get the number and the score</h2>\n<p>Weigh the dog. Ask for a body-condition score. Photograph the waist from above. If the dog is already lame, panting at rest, or collapsing, you are in clinic territory, not a new hiking hobby.</p>\n<p>A safe loss rate often lands near 1% of body weight per week, but your veterinarian sets the target. Crash diets create scavenger panic and can cost muscle.</p>\n<h2>Audit the extras</h2>\n<p>Write every chew, training bit, and stolen crust for three days. Keep extras near 10% of calories or less. Switch to pea-sized training treats. Use vegetables only if your veterinarian says they fit — grapes never do. See <a href=\"/learning/foods-toxic-to-dogs\">toxic foods</a>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Weigh meals on a scale.</li>\n<li>Use a <a href=\"/learning/slow-feeder-bowl-benefits\">slow feeder</a> if gulping leads to begging.</li>\n<li>Walk on soft ground if joints complain; add minutes slowly.</li>\n<li>Weigh every two weeks and adjust food 5–10% if the line is flat.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Exercise that does not wreck the dog</h2>\n<p>Sniff walks beat weekend hero runs. Swimming helps some dogs and overheats others. Stairs may need to wait. An <a href=\"/learning/best-orthopedic-dog-bed\">orthopedic bed</a> and nail care support the new mileage.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/blog/dog-walk.webp\" alt=\"Overweight dog on a short leash walk with a handler holding a measured treat pouch\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<h2>Household saboteurs</h2>\n<p>Kids and partners who “just share” are the usual leak. Put a note on the treat jar. Feed the dog in a separate room if the table is a battlefield.</p>\n<h2>Shopping the support kit</h2>\n<p>Measured food, a slow feeder, a treat pouch. Browse <a href=\"/dog\">the dog aisle</a>. Skip fat-heavy chews during the loss phase.</p>\n<h2>Shop this at Petshiwü</h2>\n<p>When you are ready to put this how to help an overweight dog article into the cart, Petshiwü is a nationwide online pet store with free shipping over $49 and no autoship. NYC delivery is available now; nationwide shipping opens soon.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/dog\">Browse matching products at Petshiwü</a> — free shipping over $49, no autoship.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>How long until my dog looks slimmer?</h3>\n<p>Weeks to months. The scale and the rib check matter more than a week of guilt.</p>\n<h3>Can I exercise the weight off without changing food?</h3>\n<p>Almost never. Exercise helps. Math still wins. Joint-safe movement plus portions is the pair.</p>\n<h3>Is a fat dog just happy?</h3>\n<p>Extra weight is linked to shorter, less comfortable lives. Kindness is a leaner body, not a second dinner.</p>\n<h2>Related Petshiwü guides</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/best-dog-food-for-weight-loss\">Best Dog Food for Weight Loss: Calorie Cuts That Do Not Starve Muscle</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/how-much-to-feed-a-dog\">How Much to Feed a Dog: Calories, Body Condition, and the Lying Cup</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/best-joint-supplements-for-dogs\">Best Joint Supplements for Dogs: What Labels Can Promise and What They Cannot</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>This article is educational and is not a diagnosis or treatment plan. Ask your veterinarian before you change food, supplements, or medicine, and seek care right away for blood in stool or vomit, repeated vomiting, trouble breathing, collapse, or a pet that cannot keep water down.</em></p>",
