    "slug": "how-often-to-bathe-a-dog",
    "title": "How Often to Bathe a Dog: Coat Type, Skin Barrier, and the Dish-Soap Mistake",
    "description": "How often to bathe a dog: by coat and lifestyle, which shampoos protect the skin barrier, and when odor means infection instead of a bath night.",
    "publishedAt": "2026-05-28T10:00:00.000Z",
    "html": "<p>Dogs do not need a human shower schedule. The skin barrier has oils that cheap daily baths strip, which then makes the dog smell worse and itch more. This Petshiwü schedule is by coat and job, plus the odors that are medical, not dirty.</p>\n<figure>\n  <picture><source srcset=\"/blog/dog-grooming.webp\" type=\"image/webp\" />\n  <img src=\"/blog/dog-grooming.jpg\" alt=\"Dog standing in a tub with a measuring cup of pet shampoo, illustrating a coat-safe bath interval\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"eager\" /></picture>\n  <figcaption>Dog standing in a tub with a measuring cup of pet shampoo, illustrating a coat-safe bath interval</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h2>A starting interval, not a commandment</h2>\n<p>Many short-coated pets do well every 4–8 weeks, or when they roll in something specific. Double-coated breeds often need fewer full baths and more drying and brushing so the undercoat does not felt. Hair-coat breeds (poodles and mixes) follow the groomer’s clip calendar. Puppies can be wiped more than soaked if they get chilled.</p>\n<p>If the veterinarian prescribed a medicated shampoo, follow that interval — it overrides the lifestyle guess. See <a href=\"/learning/seasonal-allergies-in-dogs\">allergies</a> if you are bathing because of itch.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Use lukewarm water and a dog shampoo, not dish soap.</li>\n<li>Rinse until the coat squeaks — leftover soap itches.</li>\n<li>Dry double coats fully to avoid hot spots — <a href=\"/learning/dog-hot-spots-treatment\">hot spots</a>.</li>\n<li>Clean ears only if your vet showed you how; do not flood the canal.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Odor that baths will not fix</h2>\n<p>Anal-gland smell, dental infection, and yeast ears come back an hour after the towel. Book the clinic. A “funky” paw lick pattern is often allergy, not mud.</p>\n<h2>Between baths</h2>\n<p>Rinse muddy legs. Use a pet wipe on the belly after wet grass. Brush. That is 80% of clean. See <a href=\"/learning/how-to-deshed-a-dog\">deshedding</a>.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/blog/dog-grooming.webp\" alt=\"Dog standing in a tub with a measuring cup of pet shampoo, illustrating a coat-safe bath interval\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<h2>Skin conditions change the product</h2>\n<p>Oatmeal, chlorhexidine, and antifungal shampoos are different tools. Do not stack three leftover bottles. Ask which contact time the clinic wants.</p>\n<h2>Shopping a bath kit</h2>\n<p>Dog shampoo, a slicker or comb, and towels. Browse <a href=\"/dog\">the dog aisle</a>. Skip essential-oil “natural” blends unless your vet likes that exact bottle.</p>\n<h2>Shop this at Petshiwü</h2>\n<p>Skip the subscription trap and shop how often to bathe a dog gear at Petshiwü. Free shipping over $49, no autoship. NYC delivery is available now; nationwide shipping opens soon.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/dog\">Browse matching products at Petshiwü</a> — free shipping over $49, no autoship.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>Can I bathe my dog every week?</h3>\n<p>Some medicated plans say weekly. Most healthy dogs do not need it. If you must, use a gentle dog shampoo and watch for dryness.</p>\n<h3>Is human shampoo OK once?</h3>\n<p>It is a poor pH match and often too harsh. Keep a pet bottle on the shelf.</p>\n<h3>Why does my dog smell worse after a bath?</h3>\n<p>Incomplete rinse, trapped undercoat, or a skin infection. Dry thoroughly and call the vet if redness or odor stays.</p>\n<h2>Related Petshiwü guides</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/how-to-deshed-a-dog\">How to Deshed a Dog: Tools, Timing, and Why Shaving a Double Coat Backfires</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/dog-hot-spots-treatment\">Dog Hot Spots: What You Can Clean at Home and When It Is a Vet Day</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/seasonal-allergies-in-dogs\">Seasonal Allergies in Dogs: Pollen, Paws, and the Calendar Itch</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>This article is educational and is not a diagnosis or treatment plan. Ask your veterinarian before you change food, supplements, or medicine, and seek care right away for blood in stool or vomit, repeated vomiting, trouble breathing, collapse, or a pet that cannot keep water down.</em></p>",
