    "description": "Dog hot spots: how moist dermatitis starts, what you can clip and clean, and the signs that mean infection needs a veterinarian — not a kitchen cream.",
    "publishedAt": "2026-06-06T14:00:00.000Z",
    "html": "<p>A hot spot is a suddenly wet, painful, expanding patch of skin infection — often on the cheek, hip, or tail base. It can double overnight. This Petshiwü page is first aid and triage. It is not a prescription for steroids or antibiotics.</p>\n<figure>\n  <picture><source srcset=\"/blog/dog-grooming.webp\" type=\"image/webp\" />\n  <img src=\"/blog/dog-grooming.jpg\" alt=\"Close-up of a clipped circle around a dog hot spot with a vet-approved cleanser nearby\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"eager\" /></picture>\n  <figcaption>Close-up of a clipped circle around a dog hot spot with a vet-approved cleanser nearby</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h2>Why they explode</h2>\n<p>Moisture plus itch plus bacteria is the usual recipe. Allergies, fleas, a mat after a swim, or an anal-gland itch can start the lick cycle. Thick coats hide the first quarter-sized lesion until it is a saucer. See <a href=\"/learning/seasonal-allergies-in-dogs\">allergies</a> and <a href=\"/learning/how-to-deshed-a-dog\">deshedding</a> so the next one is easier to find.</p>\n<p>A hot spot on the face after swimming is common. So is one under a dirty collar.</p>\n<h2>What you may do at home if the dog allows it</h2>\n<p>Clip fur around the lesion so it can dry — only if you can do it without cutting skin. Clean with a vet-recommended chlorhexidine wipe or rinse. An e-collar stops the tongue. That is first aid, not a cure.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Do not smear antibiotic ointment meant for people unless the clinic said so.</li>\n<li>Do not use hydrogen peroxide as a daily burn.</li>\n<li>Do not wrap the spot in a wet bandage.</li>\n<li>Call the vet if the area spreads, smells like rot, or the dog is miserable.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>When it is a same-day visit</h2>\n<p>Fever, lethargy, a hot spot near the eye, or a dog that will not let you look needs the clinic. Many hot spots need prescription anti-inflammatories and sometimes antibiotics. Home “coconut oil” is food for bacteria.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/blog/dog-grooming.webp\" alt=\"Close-up of a clipped circle around a dog hot spot with a vet-approved cleanser nearby\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<h2>Stop the next one</h2>\n<p>Dry the coat after water. Keep flea prevention on. Treat the allergy if there is one. A damp undercoat after a lazy bath is a setup — see <a href=\"/learning/how-often-to-bathe-a-dog\">bathing</a>.</p>\n<h2>Shopping the prevention kit</h2>\n<p>E-collar, clipper or a groomer appointment, a vet-approved cleanser. Browse <a href=\"/dog\">the dog aisle</a> after you have a plan.</p>\n<h2>Shop this at Petshiwü</h2>\n<p>Build the dog hot spots treatment shopping list in our dog collection. Petshiwü offers free shipping over $49, no autoship, NYC delivery now, and nationwide shipping opening soon.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/dog\">Browse matching products at Petshiwü</a> — free shipping over $49, no autoship.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>Can hot spots heal without a vet?</h3>\n<p>Tiny, early spots sometimes dry with clipping and an e-collar. Spreading, painful, or facial lesions need a veterinarian. When unsure, go in.</p>\n<h3>Are hot spots contagious?</h3>\n<p>The bacteria are ordinary skin germs, not a reason to quarantine the household. The underlying itch can still be fleas, which do spread.</p>\n<h3>Should I bathe a dog with a hot spot?</h3>\n<p>Ask the clinic. A targeted clean is different from a full soak that keeps the area wet.</p>\n<h2>Related Petshiwü guides</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/how-often-to-bathe-a-dog\">How Often to Bathe a Dog: Coat Type, Skin Barrier, and the Dish-Soap Mistake</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/seasonal-allergies-in-dogs\">Seasonal Allergies in Dogs: Pollen, Paws, and the Calendar Itch</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/how-to-deshed-a-dog\">How to Deshed a Dog: Tools, Timing, and Why Shaving a Double Coat Backfires</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>This article is educational and is not a diagnosis or treatment plan. Ask your veterinarian before you change food, supplements, or medicine, and seek care right away for blood in stool or vomit, repeated vomiting, trouble breathing, collapse, or a pet that cannot keep water down.</em></p>",
