    "title": "Seasonal Allergies in Dogs: Pollen, Paws, and the Calendar Itch",
    "description": "Seasonal allergies in dogs: how to spot pollen-season itch, paw-licking patterns, home rinse habits, and when you need allergy meds from a veterinarian.",
    "publishedAt": "2026-06-09T08:00:00.000Z",
    "html": "<p>Seasonal allergy in dogs is often environmental: pollen, mold, dust mites. It looks like face-rubbing, armpit itch, and stained paws when the trees let go. Food can overlap but does not follow a maple calendar. This Petshiwü guide is pattern recognition and home rinses — diagnosis and drugs stay with your veterinarian.</p>\n<figure>\n  <picture><source srcset=\"/blog/dog-walk.webp\" type=\"image/webp\" />\n  <img src=\"/blog/dog-walk.jpg\" alt=\"Dog licking a front paw after a spring walk, with a towel rinse station by the door\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"eager\" /></picture>\n  <figcaption>Dog licking a front paw after a spring walk, with a towel rinse station by the door</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h2>The calendar is a clue</h2>\n<p>If itch explodes the same month every year, think environment. If itch is year-round with ear gunk, think food, fleas, or mite plus atopy. Keep flea prevention on anyway — flea saliva is an allergen. See <a href=\"/learning/best-flea-and-tick-prevention-for-dogs-2026\">flea prevention</a> and <a href=\"/learning/best-dog-food-for-itchy-skin\">itchy skin and food</a>.</p>\n<p>Ears are part of the same skin. Recurrent <a href=\"/learning/dog-ear-infection-signs\">ear infections</a> in August are not bad luck.</p>\n<h2>Home habits that lower the load</h2>\n<p>Wipe paws and bellies after grassy walks. A weekly vet-approved shampoo can reduce allergen on the coat. Do not invent daily dish-soap baths. Change the air filter. Keep the dog off the freshly mowed lawn if that is the trigger week.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Rinse paws in plain water; dry well between toes.</li>\n<li>Wash bedding on a hot cycle during peak weeks.</li>\n<li>Watch for hot spots after swims.</li>\n<li>Log itch scores so the clinic can see the season.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Medications are not a personality failure</h2>\n<p>Antihistamines help some dogs and disappoint many. Prescription medications and immunotherapy exist. Do not borrow a neighbor’s Apoquel. Do not start steroids from an old bottle without a current exam.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/blog/dog-walk.webp\" alt=\"Dog licking a front paw after a spring walk, with a towel rinse station by the door\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<h2>Food trials still have a place</h2>\n<p>Year-round itch plus GI signs may justify an elimination diet. Seasonal-only itch is a weaker food story. Do not rotate proteins every week “to keep it interesting.”</p>\n<h2>Shopping the rinse-and-wipe kit</h2>\n<p>Paw towels, a gentle shampoo your vet likes, flea prevention. Browse <a href=\"/dog\">the dog aisle</a>.</p>\n<h2>Shop this at Petshiwü</h2>\n<p>When you are ready to put this seasonal allergies in dogs article into the cart, Petshiwü is a nationwide online pet store with free shipping over $49 and no autoship. NYC delivery is available now; nationwide shipping opens soon.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/dog\">Browse matching products at Petshiwü</a> — free shipping over $49, no autoship.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>How do I know it is allergies and not fleas?</h3>\n<p>You may have both. Comb for fleas and keep prevention on. A veterinarian can look at the skin and ears. This article cannot diagnose atopy.</p>\n<h3>Do grain-free diets fix seasonal allergies?</h3>\n<p>No. Pollen is not a grain. See our grain-free dog article for the separate DCM conversation.</p>\n<h3>Can I use human allergy pills?</h3>\n<p>Only if your veterinarian gives a dose and a product. Some human meds are unsafe. Do not guess.</p>\n<h2>Related Petshiwü guides</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/best-dog-food-for-itchy-skin\">Best Dog Food for Itchy Skin: Food Versus Environment Versus Fleas</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/dog-ear-infection-signs\">Dog Ear Infection Signs: Smell, Head Shakes, and When to Stop Cleaning</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/best-flea-and-tick-prevention-for-dogs-2026\">Best Flea and Tick Prevention for Dogs 2026: Oral vs Topical vs Collar</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>This article is educational and is not a diagnosis or treatment plan. Ask your veterinarian before you change food, supplements, or medicine, and seek care right away for blood in stool or vomit, repeated vomiting, trouble breathing, collapse, or a pet that cannot keep water down.</em></p>",
