    "slug": "potty-training-a-puppy",
    "title": "Potty Training a Puppy: Doors, Clocks, and the Accident Cleanup That Matters",
    "description": "Potty training a puppy: a take-out schedule, marker words, enzyme cleanup, and why punishment after the fact teaches hiding, not housetraining.",
    "publishedAt": "2026-06-14T10:00:00.000Z",
    "html": "<p>Housetraining is a schedule you run, not a moral lesson the puppy fails. Puppies pee after sleep, play, and meals. Your job is to be there, mark the success, and clean the miss so the smell does not become a neon sign. This Petshiwü protocol is boring on purpose.</p>\n<figure>\n  <picture><source srcset=\"/blog/puppy.webp\" type=\"image/webp\" />\n  <img src=\"/blog/puppy.jpg\" alt=\"Puppy on a leash at a designated outdoor potty spot with a treat pouch ready\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"eager\" /></picture>\n  <figcaption>Puppy on a leash at a designated outdoor potty spot with a treat pouch ready</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h2>The clock is the trainer</h2>\n<p>Take the puppy out after every nap, every meal, every play burst, and every 30–60 minutes when they are awake as a baby. Carry them if the door is a long hallway of temptation. Stand still in one potty spot. When they go, say a quiet word and pay. Then you can play.</p>\n<p>A crate helps because most puppies will not soil their bed if the space is sized right — see <a href=\"/learning/crate-training-a-puppy\">crate training</a>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Use an enzyme cleaner, not just lemon soap.</li>\n<li>Do not rub the puppy’s nose in urine.</li>\n<li>If you did not see it happen, you missed the teaching moment — clean and move on.</li>\n<li>Log accidents; patterns show the gap in your schedule.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Pads versus outside</h2>\n<p>Pads can work in high-rises if you are consistent. They also teach that indoor absorbent surfaces are legal. If your goal is 100% outside, skip pads or use them as a short bridge you fade. Elevator trips need a leash plan — <a href=\"/learning/leash-training-a-puppy\">leash training</a>.</p>\n<h2>Regression is information</h2>\n<p>A suddenly un-housetrained puppy may have a UTI, giardia, or too much freedom. See a veterinarian if accidents come with straining or blood. Do not add more square footage as a reward for confusion.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/blog/puppy.webp\" alt=\"Puppy on a leash at a designated outdoor potty spot with a treat pouch ready\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<h2>Overnight</h2>\n<p>Limit water-and-play chaos right before bed. Set an alarm for young pups. Keep the trip boring.</p>\n<h2>Shopping the cleanup kit</h2>\n<p>Enzyme cleaner, leash, treats, crate. Browse <a href=\"/dog\">the dog aisle</a>.</p>\n<h2>Shop this at Petshiwü</h2>\n<p>Skip the subscription trap and shop potty training a puppy gear at Petshiwü. Free shipping over $49, no autoship. NYC delivery is available now; nationwide shipping opens soon.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/dog\">Browse matching products at Petshiwü</a> — free shipping over $49, no autoship.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>How long does potty training take?</h3>\n<p>Many puppies get reliable over months, not four days. Small bladders and new homes reset the clock. Consistency beats a viral “24-hour” method.</p>\n<h3>Should I use a bell on the door?</h3>\n<p>Bells can help once the puppy understands going outside. Teach them after you already have a schedule, or you will hear bells for fun.</p>\n<h3>Why does my puppy pee when they greet me?</h3>\n<p>Excitement urination is different from not being housetrained. Keep greetings low-key and toilet first. Ask a trainer if it persists.</p>\n<h2>Related Petshiwü guides</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/crate-training-a-puppy\">Crate Training a Puppy: A Nap Den, Not a Punishment Box</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/leash-training-a-puppy\">Leash Training a Puppy: Slack, Sniff Breaks, and Equipment That Does Not Choke</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/how-to-stop-puppy-biting\">How to Stop Puppy Biting: Play Rules, Teething, and the Ouch Game</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>This article is educational and is not a diagnosis or treatment plan. Ask your veterinarian before you change food, supplements, or medicine, and seek care right away for blood in stool or vomit, repeated vomiting, trouble breathing, collapse, or a pet that cannot keep water down.</em></p>",
