    "slug": "leash-training-a-puppy",
    "title": "Leash Training a Puppy: Slack, Sniff Breaks, and Equipment That Does Not Choke",
    "description": "Leash training a puppy: how to introduce a harness, reward slack, skip retractable reels at first, and keep walks shorter than the puppy’s brain.",
    "publishedAt": "2026-06-16T11:00:00.000Z",
    "html": "<p>A puppy on a leash is a baby exploring physics. Pulling is not dominance. It is “the world is out there.” This Petshiwü lesson is equipment, slack rewards, and why a 5-mile first walk creates a screamer. Reactivity later has its own page — start here so you do not teach a tight-leash fight.</p>\n<figure>\n  <picture><source srcset=\"/blog/dog-walk.webp\" type=\"image/webp\" />\n  <img src=\"/blog/dog-walk.jpg\" alt=\"Puppy walking on a loose leash in a well-fitted harness with a handler holding treats\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"eager\" /></picture>\n  <figcaption>Puppy walking on a loose leash in a well-fitted harness with a handler holding treats</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h2>Harness first for most puppies</h2>\n<p>A well-fitted Y-harness protects a growing throat better than a puppy hanging on a collar. Collars still hold ID. Introduce the harness in the kitchen with food. If the puppy freezes like a statue, you went too fast — feed for one buckle at a time.</p>\n<p>Skip retractable leashes until the puppy has a brain. Six-foot flat leashes teach a distance you can actually manage.</p>\n<h2>Pay the slack</h2>\n<p>When the leash makes a J, mark and treat at your side. When it tightens, stop walking. Wait for a glance or a step back. Then go. This is slower than being dragged to the park and faster than a year of neck-pulling. See <a href=\"/learning/dog-leash-reactivity\">leash reactivity</a> if lunging at dogs is already starting.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Keep sessions to a few minutes for young pups.</li>\n<li>Let them sniff as a reward, not as a 20-minute plant survey every three feet if you are late — mix “let’s go” with sniff breaks.</li>\n<li>Treats should be tiny — <a href=\"/learning/best-dog-treats-2026\">treats 2026</a>.</li>\n<li>If the puppy bites the leash, trade for a toy; do not play tug-of-war with the lead as the game.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Surfaces and stairs</h2>\n<p>Practice on quiet grass before a busy sidewalk. Carry the puppy down steep stairs if they are tiny. City grates scare some pups — treat for looking, not for being dragged across.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/blog/dog-walk.webp\" alt=\"Puppy walking on a loose leash in a well-fitted harness with a handler holding treats\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<h2>Socialization is not a dog pile</h2>\n<p>Watch the world. Do not let every stranger maul the puppy. One rude greeting can teach leash fear. Pair new sights with food.</p>\n<h2>Shopping a first walk kit</h2>\n<p>Y-harness, 6-foot leash, treat pouch. Browse <a href=\"/dog\">the dog aisle</a>.</p>\n<h2>Shop this at Petshiwü</h2>\n<p>Use our dog collection to continue from leash training a puppy with brands you can repurchase. Petshiwü is a nationwide online pet store: free shipping over $49, no autoship, NYC delivery now, nationwide shipping soon.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/dog\">Browse matching products at Petshiwü</a> — free shipping over $49, no autoship.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>When can a puppy start walking?</h3>\n<p>Short, gentle walks after your veterinarian clears socialization risk. Vaccination timing is a clinic question. Hallway and yard practice can start earlier.</p>\n<h3>Should I use a prong collar on a puppy?</h3>\n<p>No. Teach slack with food and a harness. Aversive collars belong in a serious conversation with a qualified professional, not a first-week puppy kit.</p>\n<h3>Why does my puppy bite the leash?</h3>\n<p>It is a toy and a teething object. Keep a real toy, keep moving, and do not jerk the puppy off their feet.</p>\n<h2>Related Petshiwü guides</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/potty-training-a-puppy\">Potty Training a Puppy: Doors, Clocks, and the Accident Cleanup That Matters</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/dog-leash-reactivity\">Dog Leash Reactivity: Why the Sidewalk Fight Starts on a Tight Line</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/how-to-teach-a-dog-recall\">How to Teach a Dog Recall: A Name That Means “Jackpot,” Not “The Fun Ended”</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>This article is educational and is not a diagnosis or treatment plan. Ask your veterinarian before you change food, supplements, or medicine, and seek care right away for blood in stool or vomit, repeated vomiting, trouble breathing, collapse, or a pet that cannot keep water down.</em></p>",
