    "description": "How to stop puppy biting: how to end the game, what toys to trade, when biting is overtired, and the rare times a bite is a veterinary or behavior red flag.",
    "publishedAt": "2026-06-18T12:00:00.000Z",
    "html": "<p>Puppy teeth are needles and puppy brains are experimental. Biting hands is usually play and teething, not a campaign for household leadership. This Petshiwü plan is how to stop being the toy without scaring the puppy into silence — and when a bite is not “just a puppy.”</p>\n<figure>\n  <picture><source srcset=\"/blog/puppy.webp\" type=\"image/webp\" />\n  <img src=\"/blog/puppy.jpg\" alt=\"Puppy redirected from a hand onto a knotted toy during a play-training session\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"eager\" /></picture>\n  <figcaption>Puppy redirected from a hand onto a knotted toy during a play-training session</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h2>You are either a toy or a person</h2>\n<p>When teeth hit skin, make a short yelp or a calm “too bad,” freeze, and end the game for 10–20 seconds. Then offer a toy. If you wrestle with your hands, you hired the puppy as a land shark. Kids should not run screaming; they should stand like trees and hand the puppy to an adult.</p>\n<p>Overtired puppies bite more. A crate nap is training — see <a href=\"/learning/crate-training-a-puppy\">crate training</a>.</p>\n<h2>Trade, do not punish</h2>\n<p>Keep chew toys in every room. Frozen wet washcloths (supervised) can soothe gums. Do not use your shoe as a chew and then act shocked. Count treat calories — <a href=\"/learning/best-dog-treats-2026\">treats</a>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>If the puppy latches, do not yank the hand out — that tears skin and makes a tug game.</li>\n<li>Offer a toy against the teeth and wait.</li>\n<li>Skip alpha rolls and holding the mouth shut.</li>\n<li>If play escalates to hard bites and a stiff body, stop and call a trainer or behaviorist.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Teething timeline</h2>\n<p>Adult teeth arrive over months. Biting should soften as you teach. A six-month-old still shredding wrists needs a tighter plan, not a bigger lecture.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/blog/puppy.webp\" alt=\"Puppy redirected from a hand onto a knotted toy during a play-training session\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<h2>Red flags</h2>\n<p>Growling over food, biting to keep people away from a toy, or a bite that breaks skin on a child is professional-help territory the same week. Do not wait for “the puppy to grow out of it.”</p>\n<h2>Shopping a chew circuit</h2>\n<p>Soft toys, a firmer chew, a crate. Browse <a href=\"/dog\">the dog aisle</a>.</p>\n<h2>Shop this at Petshiwü</h2>\n<p>Petshiwü can send the bowls, foods, and extras this how to stop puppy biting article points to. Free shipping over $49 and no autoship. NYC delivery is available now; nationwide shipping opens soon.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/dog\">Browse matching products at Petshiwü</a> — free shipping over $49, no autoship.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>Will my puppy stop biting on their own?</h3>\n<p>Intensity usually drops with age and teaching. It drops faster if you stop playing with your hands and keep a nap schedule.</p>\n<h3>Should I use bitter spray on my hands?</h3>\n<p>It is a weak plan. Teach an off-switch and a toy. Sprays on skin can be licked off and taste terrible for everyone.</p>\n<h3>Is biting a sign of aggression in an 8-week-old?</h3>\n<p>Usually not. Context matters. Hard, frozen, silent bites are different from floppy play. When unsure, film it and show a professional.</p>\n<h2>Related Petshiwü guides</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/crate-training-a-puppy\">Crate Training a Puppy: A Nap Den, Not a Punishment Box</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/best-dog-treats-2026\">Best Dog Treats 2026: Training Bits, Chews, and the 10% Rule</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/how-to-stop-dog-barking\">How to Stop Dog Barking: Name the Job, Then Change the Payoff</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>This article is educational and is not a diagnosis or treatment plan. Ask your veterinarian before you change food, supplements, or medicine, and seek care right away for blood in stool or vomit, repeated vomiting, trouble breathing, collapse, or a pet that cannot keep water down.</em></p>",
