    "slug": "litter-box-training-a-kitten",
    "title": "Litter Box Training a Kitten: Location, Sides, and the One-Litter Rule",
    "description": "Litter box training a kitten: how many boxes, unscented litter, low sides, and why accidents are usually a box problem — or a medical one — not spite.",
    "publishedAt": "2026-06-28T09:00:00.000Z",
    "html": "<p>Most kittens already know what a box is for. Your job is to make the box easier than the rug. This Petshiwü setup is location, litter type, and the medical exceptions. Punishment after an accident teaches the kitten to hide from you, not to aim better.</p>\n<figure>\n  <picture><source srcset=\"/blog/kitten.webp\" type=\"image/webp\" />\n  <img src=\"/blog/kitten.jpg\" alt=\"Kitten stepping into a low-sided litter box with unscented clumping litter\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"eager\" /></picture>\n  <figcaption>Kitten stepping into a low-sided litter box with unscented clumping litter</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h2>Put the box where a kitten can win</h2>\n<p>Low sides for short legs. One box on each floor. Quiet corner, not next to a booming dryer. After meals and naps, set the kitten in the box and praise a success. If you adopted a weanling who never saw a box, show them once — do not scrub their paws in it.</p>\n<p>Unscented clumping litter is a common win if the kitten is not eating it. If they graze, use a non-clumping or clinic-approved type until that stops. See <a href=\"/learning/best-cat-litter-2026\">litter 2026</a>.</p>\n<h2>Scoop like the kitten is a food critic</h2>\n<p>Dirty boxes get rejected. Scoop daily. If accidents start, clean with an enzyme product and add a box rather than a lecture.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Keep food away from the box.</li>\n<li>Do not use a covered box as the only option for a tiny kitten.</li>\n<li>If they cry in the box, think pain — <a href=\"/learning/cat-uti-symptoms\">urinary signs</a>.</li>\n<li>Play after a successful pee so the box predicts good things, not exile.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Self-cleaning units and kittens</h2>\n<p>Weight sensors may miss a 2-pound kitten. Keep a simple open box until they are heavier. See <a href=\"/learning/self-cleaning-litter-box-guide\">automatic boxes</a>.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/blog/kitten.webp\" alt=\"Kitten stepping into a low-sided litter box with unscented clumping litter\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<h2>When it is not training</h2>\n<p>Diarrhea, straining, and blood are veterinary. So is a kitten who suddenly stops using a box they already understood.</p>\n<h2>Shopping a first box kit</h2>\n<p>Low box, unscented litter, enzyme cleaner. Browse <a href=\"/cat\">the cat aisle</a>.</p>\n<h2>Shop this at Petshiwü</h2>\n<p>Petshiwü stocks everyday supplies that pair with litter box training a kitten, without an autoship contract. Free shipping over $49. NYC delivery is available now, and nationwide shipping opens soon — start in our cat collection.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/cat\">Browse matching products at Petshiwü</a> — free shipping over $49, no autoship.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>How long does litter training take?</h3>\n<p>Many kittens are reliable in days if the box is easy. Accidents mean you change the setup, not the kitten’s morals.</p>\n<h3>Can I use the same box for two kittens?</h3>\n<p>You can, and you should still have more than one box. The plus-one rule prevents bullying at the bathroom.</p>\n<h3>Should I use puppy pads for a kitten?</h3>\n<p>They teach indoor peeing on fabric. A real box is clearer unless a veterinarian asked for a temporary pad.</p>\n<h2>Related Petshiwü guides</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/best-cat-litter-2026\">Best Cat Litter 2026: Clump, Dust, and the Two-Box Rule</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/how-to-stop-cats-scratching-furniture\">How to Stop Cats Scratching Furniture: Redirect, Don’t Declaw the Problem</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/best-kitten-food-2026\">Best Kitten Food 2026: Growth Calories, DHA, and Weaning Off Queen’s Milk</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>This article is educational and is not a diagnosis or treatment plan. Ask your veterinarian before you change food, supplements, or medicine, and seek care right away for blood in stool or vomit, repeated vomiting, trouble breathing, collapse, or a pet that cannot keep water down.</em></p>",
