    "title": "How to Introduce a Dog and a Cat: Scent, Doors, and No “Let Them Work It Out”",
    "description": "How to introduce a dog and a cat: a scent-swap week, leash greetings, escape routes for the cat, and the signs you should slow down or call a professional.",
    "publishedAt": "2026-07-03T11:00:00.000Z",
    "html": "<p>A good dog-cat introduction is a week of boring, not a viral first-day video. The cat needs vertical escape. The dog needs a leash and a brain. This Petshiwü protocol is scent, sight, then short meetings. “They will work it out” is how cats get chased into not using the box.</p>\n<figure>\n  <picture><source srcset=\"/blog/indoor-cat.webp\" type=\"image/webp\" />\n  <img src=\"/blog/indoor-cat.jpg\" alt=\"Baby gate separating a leashed dog from a cat on a high perch during a calm introduction\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"eager\" /></picture>\n  <figcaption>Baby gate separating a leashed dog from a cat on a high perch during a calm introduction</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h2>Scent first, faces later</h2>\n<p>Swap blankets for several days. Feed on opposite sides of a closed door. If either animal stops eating, you are too fast. The cat’s litter box and food must stay where the dog cannot throw a party — use a baby gate with a cat-sized opening.</p>\n<p>A resident cat who suddenly scratches the sofa or pees outside the box is voting on the new roommate. See <a href=\"/learning/how-to-stop-cats-scratching-furniture\">scratching</a> and box setup.</p>\n<h2>First sight on a leash</h2>\n<p>Dog on leash, cat free to leave to a high perch. Pay the dog for looking at you, not for staring a hole in the cat. End while everyone is still polite. Thirty seconds of calm beats five minutes of a lunge.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Keep the dog’s recall and crate as safety tools — <a href=\"/learning/crate-training-an-adult-dog\">adult crate</a>.</li>\n<li>Never hold the cat in the dog’s face.</li>\n<li>If the dog screams at the gate, increase distance and hire help.</li>\n<li>Watch for a cat that freezes and stops grooming — that is not “fine.”</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Prey drive is a real limiter</h2>\n<p>Some sighthounds and terriers need management for life: gates, never unsupervised. That is responsible, not a failure. A trainer who shrugs at a hard stare is the wrong trainer.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/blog/indoor-cat.webp\" alt=\"Baby gate separating a leashed dog from a cat on a high perch during a calm introduction\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<h2>The other direction: cat into a dog house</h2>\n<p>Same rules. The dog should not lose their bed and routine on the same day. Extra walks help the dog settle. Extra vertical space helps the cat.</p>\n<h2>Shopping the intro kit</h2>\n<p>Baby gate, leash, cat tree, extra box. Browse <a href=\"/products\">products</a>, <a href=\"/dog\">dog</a>, and <a href=\"/cat\">cat</a> aisles.</p>\n<h2>Shop this at Petshiwü</h2>\n<p>Browse Petshiwü. Free shipping over $49, no autoship. NYC delivery now; nationwide shipping soon.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/products\">Browse matching products at Petshiwü</a> — free shipping over $49, no autoship.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>How long should a dog-cat introduction take?</h3>\n<p>Days to weeks. Some pairs need months of management. Anyone who says “an hour” is guessing with your pets’ bodies.</p>\n<h3>Can I leave them alone after one good meeting?</h3>\n<p>No. Unsupervised time comes after many calm, boring sessions and a plan for doors.</p>\n<h3>What if the cat hisses?</h3>\n<p>A hiss is information. Give more space. A chase or a bite is a bigger reset. Do not punish the cat for saying no.</p>\n<h2>Related Petshiwü guides</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/crate-training-an-adult-dog\">Crate Training an Adult Dog: Repairing a Bad History Without Forcing the Door</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/how-to-stop-cats-scratching-furniture\">How to Stop Cats Scratching Furniture: Redirect, Don’t Declaw the Problem</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/litter-box-training-a-kitten\">Litter Box Training a Kitten: Location, Sides, and the One-Litter Rule</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>This article is educational and is not a diagnosis or treatment plan. Ask your veterinarian before you change food, supplements, or medicine, and seek care right away for blood in stool or vomit, repeated vomiting, trouble breathing, collapse, or a pet that cannot keep water down.</em></p>",
