    "title": "Crate Training an Adult Dog: Repairing a Bad History Without Forcing the Door",
    "description": "Crate training an adult dog: how to rebuild the crate as a rest place, when to skip the crate, and why shutting a panicked adult in is not “being consistent.”",
    "publishedAt": "2026-07-05T12:00:00.000Z",
    "html": "<p>Adult dogs arrive with opinions. Some love crates. Some have been locked in one for punishment or for ten-hour days. This Petshiwü rebuild is optional-door training, not a weekend of crying it out. If the dog injures themselves, the crate is the wrong tool today — see <a href=\"/learning/dog-separation-anxiety-help\">separation anxiety</a>.</p>\n<figure>\n  <picture><source srcset=\"/blog/dog-bed.webp\" type=\"image/webp\" />\n  <img src=\"/blog/dog-bed.jpg\" alt=\"Adult dog choosing to lie in an open crate beside a couch, showing a voluntary den\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"eager\" /></picture>\n  <figcaption>Adult dog choosing to lie in an open crate beside a couch, showing a voluntary den</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h2>Make the crate a cafe, not a jail</h2>\n<p>Park it in the living room with the door tied open. Toss meals toward, then inside. Sit nearby and ignore. When the dog lies in the crate, pay. You are buying the choice. A comfortable bed helps — <a href=\"/learning/best-dog-beds-2026\">dog beds</a> — as long as it cannot be shredded into a blockage.</p>\n<p>If the dog will not enter, feed at the threshold for a week. There is no prize for slamming the latch on night one.</p>\n<h2>Close the door in tiny slices</h2>\n<p>Close it, feed through the wires, open it. Build to a minute while you sit there, then while you stand, then while you leave the room for five seconds. Video the dog. Pacing and drooling mean you jumped ahead.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Never use the crate as punishment after a mess.</li>\n<li>Give a chew only if it is safe and calorie-counted.</li>\n<li>A cover can help some dogs and scare others — test.</li>\n<li>If teeth hit metal, stop and call a professional.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>When to use a room instead</h2>\n<p>A gated kitchen with a <a href=\"/learning/best-dog-beds-2026\">bed</a> is a valid adult plan. Not every dog needs a crate to be a good housemate. Travel crates can be trained later for vet visits.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/blog/dog-bed.webp\" alt=\"Adult dog choosing to lie in an open crate beside a couch, showing a voluntary den\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<h2>Rescues and unknown histories</h2>\n<p>Go slower. Pair the crate with the best food. If the history includes hoarding in a crate, you may never use one at home, and that can still be a good life.</p>\n<h2>Shopping a rebuild kit</h2>\n<p>Right-size crate, washable bed, treats. Browse <a href=\"/dog\">the dog aisle</a>.</p>\n<h2>Shop this at Petshiwü</h2>\n<p>Petshiwü can send the bowls, foods, and extras this crate training an adult dog article points to. Free shipping over $49 and no autoship. NYC delivery is available now; nationwide shipping opens soon.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/dog\">Browse matching products at Petshiwü</a> — free shipping over $49, no autoship.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>How long to crate-train an adult?</h3>\n<p>Days to months depending on history. Faster is not kinder.</p>\n<h3>Should I crate at night from day one?</h3>\n<p>Only if the dog already rests there. Otherwise start with the crate as a day den and use a gated room at night.</p>\n<h3>Can a crate fix chewing?</h3>\n<p>It can prevent chewing while you are gone if the dog is already comfortable. It does not teach what to chew. Provide legal outlets and exercise.</p>\n<h2>Related Petshiwü guides</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/crate-training-a-puppy\">Crate Training a Puppy: A Nap Den, Not a Punishment Box</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/dog-separation-anxiety-help\">Dog Separation Anxiety Help: Panic Versus Boredom and a Real Exit Plan</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/best-dog-beds-2026\">Best Dog Beds 2026: Fill, Covers, and a Size That Fits the Curl</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>This article is educational and is not a diagnosis or treatment plan. Ask your veterinarian before you change food, supplements, or medicine, and seek care right away for blood in stool or vomit, repeated vomiting, trouble breathing, collapse, or a pet that cannot keep water down.</em></p>",
