    "description": "How to teach a dog recall: how to charge a cue, long-line practice, the mistakes that poison “come,” and when a recall is not ready for off-leash parks.",
    "publishedAt": "2026-07-08T13:00:00.000Z",
    "html": "<p>Recall is a life jacket. It is also the cue people poison by calling the dog to end the park, clip nails, or scold. This Petshiwü method is a new word if “come” is already junk, a long line, and payments that beat the squirrel. Off-leash freedom is earned, not assumed.</p>\n<figure>\n  <picture><source srcset=\"/blog/dog-training.webp\" type=\"image/webp\" />\n  <img src=\"/blog/dog-training.jpg\" alt=\"Dog running toward a handler on a long line in an open field for a recall drill\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"eager\" /></picture>\n  <figcaption>Dog running toward a handler on a long line in an open field for a recall drill</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h2>Charge a word that has no baggage</h2>\n<p>Pick “here” or a whistle. Say it once, then show a handful of excellent food. Repeat in the house. Never attach it to a bath you have not trained. Use a marker if you like — <a href=\"/learning/clicker-training-for-dogs\">clicker training</a>.</p>\n<p>If the dog ignores you, you are not being disrespected. You are being outbid. Raise the pay or lower the distraction.</p>\n<h2>Long line before off leash</h2>\n<p>A 20–30 foot line in a quiet field lets you practice without losing the dog. Call once. Cheer. Pay like you won the lottery. Then release them back to sniff so recall does not mean “party over.” See <a href=\"/learning/best-dog-treats-2026\">treats</a> for pocket food that travels.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Do not call if you cannot enforce kindly on the line.</li>\n<li>Practice when the dog is already coming toward you sometimes — catch success.</li>\n<li>Kids should not chant the cue 15 times.</li>\n<li>A failed off-leash recall is information: go back to the line.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Poisoned cues</h2>\n<p>If “come” means nail trim, start a new cue. Do the trim with a different word and a lot of cheese after. Recall should predict good news more often than not.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/blog/dog-training.webp\" alt=\"Dog running toward a handler on a long line in an open field for a recall drill\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<h2>When off-leash is a no</h2>\n<p>Recall is not ready around livestock, playgrounds, or a dog with prey drive you have not tested. Leash laws still apply. A GPS tag is backup, not a training plan — <a href=\"/learning/best-gps-dog-tracker-2026\">GPS trackers</a>.</p>\n<h2>Shopping a recall kit</h2>\n<p>Long line, harness, treat pouch. Browse <a href=\"/dog\">the dog aisle</a>.</p>\n<h2>Shop this at Petshiwü</h2>\n<p>Ready to restock after reading about how to teach a dog recall? Petshiwü ships as a nationwide online pet store with free shipping over $49 and no autoship. NYC delivery is available now; nationwide shipping opens soon.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/dog\">Browse matching products at Petshiwü</a> — free shipping over $49, no autoship.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>How long until recall is reliable?</h3>\n<p>Reliable in the kitchen can be days. Reliable at the park can be months. Distraction is the exam.</p>\n<h3>Should I use a shock collar for recall?</h3>\n<p>We recommend skilled positive training and a long line first. Pain can suppress and also create fallout. Talk to a qualified professional if you are considering aversive tools.</p>\n<h3>Why does my dog come at home but not at the park?</h3>\n<p>The park pays better. Practice closer, use better food, and add distance slowly. Do not test a new recall next to a soccer game.</p>\n<h2>Related Petshiwü guides</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/clicker-training-for-dogs\">Clicker Training for Dogs: A Marker You Can Trust More Than “Good Boy”</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/leash-training-a-puppy\">Leash Training a Puppy: Slack, Sniff Breaks, and Equipment That Does Not Choke</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/best-dog-treats-2026\">Best Dog Treats 2026: Training Bits, Chews, and the 10% Rule</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>This article is educational and is not a diagnosis or treatment plan. Ask your veterinarian before you change food, supplements, or medicine, and seek care right away for blood in stool or vomit, repeated vomiting, trouble breathing, collapse, or a pet that cannot keep water down.</em></p>",
