    "description": "Best dog beds 2026: how to measure for a curl or a sprawl, bolus vs orthopedic foam, washable covers, and when a crate pad is the better bed.",
    "publishedAt": "2026-07-10T14:00:00.000Z",
    "html": "<p>A dog bed is furniture that will be drooled on. In 2026 the options are memory foam, shredded fill, cooling mats, and designer pillows that flatten in a month. This Petshiwü buyer’s guide is measurement, washability, and matching the bed to joints and chewing — not a color story.</p>\n<figure>\n  <picture><source srcset=\"/blog/dog-bed.webp\" type=\"image/webp\" />\n  <img src=\"/blog/dog-bed.jpg\" alt=\"Medium dog curled in a bolster bed next to a tape measure showing correct bed diameter\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"eager\" /></picture>\n  <figcaption>Medium dog curled in a bolster bed next to a tape measure showing correct bed diameter</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h2>Measure the dog, not the Instagram corner</h2>\n<p>Have the dog lie in their usual sleep shape. Measure nose to tail base and add room if they sprawl. A bolster bed suits curlers who like a chin rest. A flat mat suits belly-sleepers and crates. If the bed is for a crate, it must fit without bunching — see <a href=\"/learning/crate-training-a-puppy\">crate sizing</a>.</p>\n<p>Seniors and heavy breeds should look at <a href=\"/learning/best-orthopedic-dog-bed\">orthopedic foam</a> rather than a cute doughnut that bottoms out.</p>\n<h2>Fill and covers decide the second year</h2>\n<p>Shredded fill lumps. Solid foam keeps shape if the density is real. A zip cover you can wash is not optional in a real house. Waterproof liners help puppies and incontinent seniors.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Skip beds the dog can unzip and eat.</li>\n<li>Cooling mats help some dogs in summer and feel awful to others — offer a choice.</li>\n<li>Elevated cots keep air under a hot dog and can be slick for seniors.</li>\n<li>If the dog ignores the bed, try moving it to where they already sleep.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Chewers and the expensive snack</h2>\n<p>A stuffed bed is a blockage risk for shredders. Use a cot, a rubber mat, or a bed you can afford to replace while you train. See <a href=\"/learning/best-toys-for-aggressive-chewers\">chewer toys</a> so the bed is not the toy.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/blog/dog-bed.webp\" alt=\"Medium dog curled in a bolster bed next to a tape measure showing correct bed diameter\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<h2>Apartment realities</h2>\n<p>One bed by the couch and one crate pad reduce roaming accidents at night. See <a href=\"/learning/best-dogs-for-apartments\">apartment dogs</a> for the rest of the lifestyle fit.</p>\n<h2>Shopping a bed you will wash</h2>\n<p>Measure twice. Browse beds in <a href=\"/dog\">the dog aisle</a>. We will not invent a 2026 lab comfort score.</p>\n<h2>Shop this at Petshiwü</h2>\n<p>Build the best dog beds shopping list in our dog collection. Petshiwü offers free shipping over $49, no autoship, NYC delivery now, and nationwide shipping opening soon.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/dog\">Browse matching products at Petshiwü</a> — free shipping over $49, no autoship.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>How often should I replace a dog bed?</h3>\n<p>When it smells through a wash, sags so joints hit the floor, or is unsafe to chew. Foam lasts longer than cheap fiberfill.</p>\n<h3>Do dogs need a bed if they sleep on the couch?</h3>\n<p>They need a legal, washable place that is theirs. A bed can also be the off-switch spot you train.</p>\n<h3>Are orthopedic beds only for old dogs?</h3>\n<p>They help any dog on hard floors, especially large and giant breeds. Age is not the only reason.</p>\n<h2>Related Petshiwü guides</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/best-orthopedic-dog-bed\">Best Orthopedic Dog Bed: Foam Density, Joints, and a Cover You Can Wash</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/crate-training-a-puppy\">Crate Training a Puppy: A Nap Den, Not a Punishment Box</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/best-dogs-for-apartments\">Best Dogs for Apartments: Energy, Voice, and the Elevator Reality</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>This article is educational and is not a diagnosis or treatment plan. Ask your veterinarian before you change food, supplements, or medicine, and seek care right away for blood in stool or vomit, repeated vomiting, trouble breathing, collapse, or a pet that cannot keep water down.</em></p>",
