    "description": "Slow feeder bowl benefits: how maze bowls slow eating, bloat-aware habits, puzzle difficulty, and when a slow feeder is the wrong tool for a flat-faced dog.",
    "publishedAt": "2026-07-17T10:00:00.000Z",
    "html": "<p>A slow feeder is a bowl with a maze so the dog cannot inhale dinner in eight seconds. That can mean less gulping, less begging for a second illusion meal, and a job for a busy brain. It is not proven bloat insurance. This Petshiwü guide is how to pick a maze that works, not one that makes a brachycephalic dog give up.</p>\n<figure>\n  <picture><source srcset=\"/blog/kibble-bowl.webp\" type=\"image/webp\" />\n  <img src=\"/blog/kibble-bowl.jpg\" alt=\"Dog eating kibble from a maze slow-feeder bowl instead of a deep open dish\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"eager\" /></picture>\n  <figcaption>Dog eating kibble from a maze slow-feeder bowl instead of a deep open dish</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h2>Why speed is a household problem</h2>\n<p>Fast eaters swallow air, ask for more, and sometimes regurgitate. Spreading kibble in a muffin tin or a maze bowl stretches the meal. You still feed the same grams — see <a href=\"/learning/how-much-to-feed-a-dog\">how much to feed</a>. If two dogs compete, feed them apart. Competition is a common reason for speed.</p>\n<p>Deep-chested breeds should still avoid a huge meal plus hard play. A maze is an extra habit, not a surgical opinion.</p>\n<h2>Pick a difficulty the dog can finish</h2>\n<p>Start with a shallow maze. If the dog flips the bowl, get a heavier one or a silicone mat that grips. Flat-faced dogs need wider, shallower patterns so they can breathe while they work. Frustration that lasts 40 minutes is not enrichment — it is skipped dinner.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Wash mazes well; grease hides in ridges.</li>\n<li>Count wet food in a lick mat if the maze is for kibble only — <a href=\"/learning/lick-mat-benefits-for-dogs\">lick mats</a>.</li>\n<li>If the dog paws so hard they injure a nail, lower the difficulty.</li>\n<li>A <a href=\"/learning/best-puzzle-toys-for-dogs\">puzzle toy</a> can replace a meal bowl some days.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What slow feeders do not do</h2>\n<p>They do not replace a veterinary visit for chronic vomiting. They do not treat food aggression by themselves — that needs a behavior plan and separate feeding.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/blog/kibble-bowl.webp\" alt=\"Dog eating kibble from a maze slow-feeder bowl instead of a deep open dish\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<h2>DIY versions</h2>\n<p>A baking tray with scattered kibble works on a travel day. Tennis balls in a bowl are a hack if they cannot be swallowed. Supervise any DIY.</p>\n<h2>Shopping a maze you will wash</h2>\n<p>Dishwasher-safe if possible. Browse <a href=\"/dog\">the dog aisle</a>.</p>\n<h2>Shop this at Petshiwü</h2>\n<p>Skip the subscription trap and shop slow feeder bowl benefits gear at Petshiwü. Free shipping over $49, no autoship. NYC delivery is available now; nationwide shipping opens soon.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/dog\">Browse matching products at Petshiwü</a> — free shipping over $49, no autoship.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>Do slow feeders prevent bloat?</h3>\n<p>They may reduce gulping. They are not a guarantee against GDV. Ask your veterinarian about meal size, exercise timing, and breed risk.</p>\n<h3>Can puppies use slow feeders?</h3>\n<p>Yes, with an easy pattern and a watchful eye. Very young pups may need a simple dish.</p>\n<h3>My dog won’t eat from the maze. Now what?</h3>\n<p>Too hard, or the food is not worth it. Go easier, add a topper your vet is fine with, or scatter-feed on a mat.</p>\n<h2>Related Petshiwü guides</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/how-much-to-feed-a-dog\">How Much to Feed a Dog: Calories, Body Condition, and the Lying Cup</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/best-puzzle-toys-for-dogs\">Best Puzzle Toys for Dogs: Difficulty Levels That Do Not Create a Genius Criminal</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/best-automatic-dog-feeder\">Best Automatic Dog Feeder: Portions, Backup Power, and Multi-Dog Chaos</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>This article is educational and is not a diagnosis or treatment plan. Ask your veterinarian before you change food, supplements, or medicine, and seek care right away for blood in stool or vomit, repeated vomiting, trouble breathing, collapse, or a pet that cannot keep water down.</em></p>",
