    "slug": "lick-mat-benefits-for-dogs",
    "title": "Lick Mat Benefits for Dogs: Calm, Baths, and a Frozen Job for the Mouth",
    "description": "Lick mat benefits for dogs: how licking can lower arousal, how to freeze toppers, bath-time use, and hygiene so the mat does not become a bacteria tray.",
    "publishedAt": "2026-07-22T12:00:00.000Z",
    "html": "<p>A lick mat is a textured rubber sheet you smear with food. The repetitive licking can occupy a mouth that would otherwise bark, bite a hose, or pace. This Petshiwü guide is how to use one for baths, crates, and fireworks prep — and how to wash it so you are not serving last week’s fat.</p>\n<figure>\n  <picture><source srcset=\"/blog/dog-toys.webp\" type=\"image/webp\" />\n  <img src=\"/blog/dog-toys.jpg\" alt=\"Frozen lick mat smeared with yogurt-safe topping stuck to a bathtub wall during a bath\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"eager\" /></picture>\n  <figcaption>Frozen lick mat smeared with yogurt-safe topping stuck to a bathtub wall during a bath</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h2>Why licking is a useful behavior</h2>\n<p>Licking is a natural self-soothing action for many dogs. A mat gives that action a legal target. It is not sedation and it is not a treatment for separation panic. It can be a piece of <a href=\"/learning/fireworks-anxiety-in-dogs\">fireworks planning</a> or a bath distraction — <a href=\"/learning/how-often-to-bathe-a-dog\">bathing</a>.</p>\n<p>Suction cups on a tub wall keep the mat still. If they fail, the mat becomes a skateboard. Supervise.</p>\n<h2>What to smear — and what not to</h2>\n<p>Plain yogurt without xylitol, a bit of complete wet food, or a pet-safe spread. Peanut butter only if it is xylitol-free and calorie-counted. Freeze the mat to make it last. A puppy can use a thinner smear so they do not eat a day’s calories in one bath.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Wash in hot soapy water after every use.</li>\n<li>Replace mats that crack and harbor gunk.</li>\n<li>Do not leave a guarding dog alone with a high-value mat if another pet can approach.</li>\n<li>If diarrhea starts, the topping was too rich.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Crate and travel</h2>\n<p>A frozen mat can help a crate session if the crate is already a rest place. It can also create a mess. Use a tray. See crate guides if the dog is panicked, not bored.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/blog/dog-toys.webp\" alt=\"Frozen lick mat smeared with yogurt-safe topping stuck to a bathtub wall during a bath\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<h2>Puzzles versus mats</h2>\n<p>Mats are low-frustration. Sliding puzzles are higher-frustration. Choose based on the dog’s state that day — <a href=\"/learning/best-puzzle-toys-for-dogs\">puzzles</a>.</p>\n<h2>Shopping a mat that sticks</h2>\n<p>Deep texture, suction if you need it. Browse <a href=\"/dog\">the dog aisle</a>.</p>\n<h2>Shop this at Petshiwü</h2>\n<p>Petshiwü can send the bowls, foods, and extras this lick mat benefits for dogs article points to. Free shipping over $49 and no autoship. NYC delivery is available now; nationwide shipping opens soon.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/dog\">Browse matching products at Petshiwü</a> — free shipping over $49, no autoship.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>Are lick mats good for anxious dogs?</h3>\n<p>They can help some dogs settle in a managed setting. They do not treat a panic disorder. Pair with a behavior plan when needed.</p>\n<h3>Can I use human leftovers?</h3>\n<p>Only if every ingredient is dog-safe. No onion, no xylitol, no grapes. When unsure, use the dog’s own wet food.</p>\n<h3>How often can I offer a lick mat?</h3>\n<p>As often as the calories fit. Daily is fine for many dogs if dinner shrinks to match.</p>\n<h2>Related Petshiwü guides</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/best-puzzle-toys-for-dogs\">Best Puzzle Toys for Dogs: Difficulty Levels That Do Not Create a Genius Criminal</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/fireworks-anxiety-in-dogs\">Fireworks Anxiety in Dogs: A July Plan That Starts Before Dusk</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/how-often-to-bathe-a-dog\">How Often to Bathe a Dog: Coat Type, Skin Barrier, and the Dish-Soap Mistake</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>This article is educational and is not a diagnosis or treatment plan. Ask your veterinarian before you change food, supplements, or medicine, and seek care right away for blood in stool or vomit, repeated vomiting, trouble breathing, collapse, or a pet that cannot keep water down.</em></p>",
