    "title": "Traveling with a Dog: Cars, Hotels, and a Packing List That Includes the Vet",
    "description": "Traveling with a dog: crate training for the car, hotel etiquette, health certificates, and how to pack food so a stomach does not ruin the trip.",
    "publishedAt": "2026-07-29T08:00:00.000Z",
    "html": "<p>A good dog trip is logistics, not a vibe. Cars overheat. Hotels have leftover chicken on the carpet. This Petshiwü checklist is records, restraint, food, and the stops that keep a bladder and a brain intact. Flying has extra rules — cats get their own page, and dogs in cargo need a conversation you start weeks early.</p>\n<figure>\n  <picture><source srcset=\"/blog/pet-travel.webp\" type=\"image/webp\" />\n  <img src=\"/blog/pet-travel.jpg\" alt=\"Packed dog travel kit with collapsible bowls, a leash, records, and a crate by a car door\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"eager\" /></picture>\n  <figcaption>Packed dog travel kit with collapsible bowls, a leash, records, and a crate by a car door</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h2>The car is a climate and a seat belt</h2>\n<p>Never leave a dog in a closed car. Restrain with a crash-tested crate or harness; an unrestrained dog is a projectile. Practice crate-in-car on a 10-minute drive before a six-hour one. Crack windows are not air conditioning.</p>\n<p>Stop every few hours for water and a potty. A <a href=\"/learning/best-gps-dog-tracker-2026\">tracker</a> helps at chaotic rest areas. Keep the leash on before the door opens.</p>\n<h2>Hotels and rentals</h2>\n<p>Book pet-friendly on purpose. Bring a bed that smells like home, a lint roller, and a crate for when you go to dinner. Do not leave a barking dog to meet the manager. Wipe muddy paws. Tip for extra cleanup if you made extra cleanup.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Pack the usual food — new food plus stress is diarrhea.</li>\n<li>Bring a copy of vaccines and your vet’s number.</li>\n<li>Know an emergency clinic along the route.</li>\n<li>Ask about breed or weight rules before you arrive.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Crossing borders and flying</h2>\n<p>Health certificates have expiration windows. Start early. Cabin versus cargo is a size and airline policy question. If you fly, train the carrier like a den. Cats: <a href=\"/learning/flying-with-a-cat\">flying with a cat</a>.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/blog/pet-travel.webp\" alt=\"Packed dog travel kit with collapsible bowls, a leash, records, and a crate by a car door\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<h2>Food safety on the road</h2>\n<p>Keep kibble sealed. Keep raw food cold or leave it at home — see recall and raw-safety habits. A backup bag matters if you spill the only food at a rest stop.</p>\n<h2>Shopping a travel kit</h2>\n<p>Collapsible bowls, leash, waste bags, familiar bed. Browse <a href=\"/dog\">the dog aisle</a>.</p>\n<h2>Shop this at Petshiwü</h2>\n<p>When you are ready to put this traveling with a dog article into the cart, Petshiwü is a nationwide online pet store with free shipping over $49 and no autoship. NYC delivery is available now; nationwide shipping opens soon.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/dog\">Browse matching products at Petshiwü</a> — free shipping over $49, no autoship.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>Can I take my dog on a road trip the first week I have them?</h3>\n<p>Short trips yes, epic trips no. Build the car as a calm place first.</p>\n<h3>Do I need a muzzle for travel?</h3>\n<p>Only if your veterinarian or trainer recommended one and the dog is trained to wear it comfortably. It is not a substitute for a crate plan.</p>\n<h3>What if my dog gets carsick?</h3>\n<p>Ask the clinic. Withhold a huge meal right before driving, keep the crate secure, and do not let the dog be a dashboard ornament.</p>\n<h2>Related Petshiwü guides</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/flying-with-a-cat\">Flying with a Cat: Carrier Training, Airline Rules, and Sedation Myths</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/best-gps-dog-tracker-2026\">Best GPS Dog Tracker 2026: Coverage, Battery, and What a Tag Cannot Do</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/how-to-check-dog-food-recalls\">How to Check Dog Food Recalls: FDA, Lot Codes, and What to Do Tonight</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>This article is educational and is not a diagnosis or treatment plan. Ask your veterinarian before you change food, supplements, or medicine, and seek care right away for blood in stool or vomit, repeated vomiting, trouble breathing, collapse, or a pet that cannot keep water down.</em></p>",
