    "title": "Is Pet Insurance Worth It in 2026? Deductibles, Waiting Periods, and What Is Excluded",
    "description": "Is pet insurance worth it in 2026? How to compare accident-only vs wellness, waiting periods, exclusions, and when a savings account is the honest alternative.",
    "publishedAt": "2026-08-03T10:00:00.000Z",
    "html": "<p>Pet insurance is a financial product, not a wellness spell. In 2026 policies still hinge on waiting periods, pre-existing exclusions, and whether you want accident-only or a broader medical plan. This Petshiwü explainer is how to read a sample policy. We do not sell insurance and we will not invent a company ranking.</p>\n<figure>\n  <picture><source srcset=\"/blog/pet-travel.webp\" type=\"image/webp\" />\n  <img src=\"/blog/pet-travel.jpg\" alt=\"Pet medical invoice and an insurance policy summary side by side on a kitchen table\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"eager\" /></picture>\n  <figcaption>Pet medical invoice and an insurance policy summary side by side on a kitchen table</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h2>Know what you are buying</h2>\n<p>Accident-only is cheaper and ignores chronic disease. Comprehensive medical may reimburse a percentage after a deductible. Wellness riders are prepaid routine care — run the math against your actual vaccine and dental bills. A cute app does not change the contract.</p>\n<p>Waiting periods mean a limp that starts in week one may be excluded forever as pre-existing. Enroll when the pet is healthy if you enroll at all.</p>\n<h2>The exclusions people miss</h2>\n<p>Breed-specific conditions, dental disease, behavior, and preventive meds are often limited. Read the list. If your dog already has ear infections every summer, that pattern may be excluded. Prevention still matters — heartworm and fleas are cheaper than claims fights. See <a href=\"/learning/heartworm-prevention-for-dogs\">heartworm</a>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Compare annual caps and per-incident caps.</li>\n<li>Ask how reimbursement works — you usually pay the vet first.</li>\n<li>Check whether your clinic is fine with the paperwork load.</li>\n<li>A dedicated savings account is a valid alternative if you will actually fund it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Emergencies that wreck uninsured budgets</h2>\n<p>Foreign bodies, toxin ingestions — <a href=\"/learning/foods-toxic-to-dogs\">toxic foods</a> — and hit-by-car bills are why some people sleep better with a policy. Others prefer a high-interest savings buffer. Both are adult plans. Neither is a reason to skip basic prevention.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/blog/pet-travel.webp\" alt=\"Pet medical invoice and an insurance policy summary side by side on a kitchen table\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<h2>Puppies, seniors, and switching</h2>\n<p>Young pets are easier to enroll. Seniors cost more and carry more exclusions. Switching companies can reset waiting periods. Read before you cancel.</p>\n<h2>Shopping is reading, not clicking an ad</h2>\n<p>Download sample policies. We will not name a “best 2026” carrier. Use the money you would have spent on a mystery wellness plan to fund a real exam if you stay uninsured.</p>\n<h2>Shop this at Petshiwü</h2>\n<p>Skip the subscription trap and shop is pet insurance worth it gear at Petshiwü. Free shipping over $49, no autoship. NYC delivery is available now; nationwide shipping opens soon.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/products\">Browse matching products at Petshiwü</a> — free shipping over $49, no autoship.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>Is pet insurance worth it?</h3>\n<p>It is worth it if a large unexpected bill would change your pet’s outcome and you understand the exclusions. It is not worth it if you will not read the policy or cannot pay the vet up front.</p>\n<h3>Does insurance cover pre-existing conditions?</h3>\n<p>Usually not. Some policies have waiting-period lookbacks. Honesty on the application matters.</p>\n<h3>Accident-only or full coverage?</h3>\n<p>Accident-only is a narrower safety net. Full coverage costs more and still has exclusions. Match the product to your risk tolerance and cash buffer.</p>\n<h2>Related Petshiwü guides</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/heartworm-prevention-for-dogs\">Heartworm Prevention for Dogs: Year-Round Chews and the Test First Rule</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/foods-toxic-to-dogs\">Foods Toxic to Dogs: The Kitchen List You Should Actually Tape Up</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/best-flea-and-tick-prevention-for-dogs-2026\">Best Flea and Tick Prevention for Dogs 2026: Oral vs Topical vs Collar</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>This article is educational and is not a diagnosis or treatment plan. Ask your veterinarian before you change food, supplements, or medicine, and seek care right away for blood in stool or vomit, repeated vomiting, trouble breathing, collapse, or a pet that cannot keep water down.</em></p>",
