    "publishedAt": "2026-08-08T12:00:00.000Z",
    "html": "<p>Aggressive chewers are not villains. They have jobs for their jaws. “Indestructible” is a marketing word that has sent many dogs to surgery. This Petshiwü guide is how to pick toys that fail in large, obvious pieces — and how to retire a toy before it becomes a blockage.</p>\n<figure>\n  <picture><source srcset=\"/blog/dog-toys.webp\" type=\"image/webp\" />\n  <img src=\"/blog/dog-toys.jpg\" alt=\"Thick rubber chew toy beside a destroyed plush, showing a safer choice for a power chewer\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"eager\" /></picture>\n  <figcaption>Thick rubber chew toy beside a destroyed plush, showing a safer choice for a power chewer</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h2>Watch the first ten minutes</h2>\n<p>Give a new toy in front of you. If the dog is shaving shards or swallowing chunks, take it. Rubber toys meant for power chewers should flex a little. Nylon bricks and antlers break teeth — ask your veterinarian if your dog already has a slab fracture history.</p>\n<p>Plush is a dissection project, not a chew. Save it for supervised tug if you use it at all.</p>\n<h2>Rotate and size up</h2>\n<p>A toy that is too small is a swallow. Size for the dog in front of you. Rotate so boredom does not send them to the orthopedic bed — <a href=\"/learning/best-dog-beds-2026\">beds</a>. Pair chews with calorie math — <a href=\"/learning/best-dog-treats-2026\">treats</a>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Inspect for sharp edges daily.</li>\n<li>Skip cooked bones and super-hard hooves if your vet has flagged teeth.</li>\n<li>Use a long-lasting rubber toy with food inside for crate time if the dog is already crate-comfortable.</li>\n<li>If you find stuffing in stool, the toy lost.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>The bed is not a chew</h2>\n<p>Shredders need cots or beds they cannot unzip. Training an “off” and providing legal chews is cheaper than a foreign-body surgery.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/blog/dog-toys.webp\" alt=\"Thick rubber chew toy beside a destroyed plush, showing a safer choice for a power chewer\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<h2>Puppies versus adults</h2>\n<p>Puppy teeth need softer options. Adult power chewers need tougher rubber. See <a href=\"/learning/how-to-stop-puppy-biting\">puppy biting</a> so hands are not the toy.</p>\n<h2>Shopping a chew that can fail safely</h2>\n<p>One rubber, one edible chew your vet likes, one supervised tug. Browse <a href=\"/dog\">the dog aisle</a>.</p>\n<h2>Shop this at Petshiwü</h2>\n<p>Petshiwü can send the bowls, foods, and extras this best toys for aggressive chewers article points to. Free shipping over $49 and no autoship. NYC delivery is available now; nationwide shipping opens soon.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/dog\">Browse matching products at Petshiwü</a> — free shipping over $49, no autoship.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>What is the safest toy for a power chewer?</h3>\n<p>The one you supervise and retire when it shrinks or shards. There is no forever-safe object.</p>\n<h3>Are antlers OK?</h3>\n<p>They are very hard and fracture teeth in some dogs. Ask your veterinarian. Many clinics would rather you pick a slightly giving rubber toy.</p>\n<h3>Why does my dog destroy toys so fast?</h3>\n<p>Genetics, boredom, and a toy that is too easy. Add exercise, rotate, and raise the toughness — without going into tooth-breaking territory.</p>\n<h2>Related Petshiwü guides</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/best-dog-treats-2026\">Best Dog Treats 2026: Training Bits, Chews, and the 10% Rule</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/best-dog-beds-2026\">Best Dog Beds 2026: Fill, Covers, and a Size That Fits the Curl</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/how-to-stop-puppy-biting\">How to Stop Puppy Biting: Play Rules, Teething, and the Ouch Game</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>This article is educational and is not a diagnosis or treatment plan. Ask your veterinarian before you change food, supplements, or medicine, and seek care right away for blood in stool or vomit, repeated vomiting, trouble breathing, collapse, or a pet that cannot keep water down.</em></p>",
