    "title": "Fourth of July Dog Safety: Escapes, Burns, and a Quiet Room Plan",
    "description": "Fourth of July dog safety: how to prevent holiday runaways, keep dogs off hot grills, and build a sound-safe room before the first firework. IDs first.",
    "publishedAt": "2026-08-10T13:00:00.000Z",
    "html": "<p>The Fourth loses more dogs than any other week. Gates open, fireworks boom, and a dog who never bolts discovers a new sport. This Petshiwü safety page is ID, containment, and food hazards on the picnic table. For the sound plan, use <a href=\"/learning/fireworks-anxiety-in-dogs\">fireworks anxiety</a> as the companion piece.</p>\n<figure>\n  <picture><source srcset=\"/blog/dog-anxiety.webp\" type=\"image/webp\" />\n  <img src=\"/blog/dog-anxiety.jpg\" alt=\"Dog wearing updated ID tags in a quiet interior room with blinds closed on the Fourth of July\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"eager\" /></picture>\n  <figcaption>Dog wearing updated ID tags in a quiet interior room with blinds closed on the Fourth of July</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h2>Assume the dog will try the gate</h2>\n<p>Update tags and the microchip app in June. Check fence lines. Do not use a backyard tie-out during a party. A <a href=\"/learning/best-gps-dog-tracker-2026\">GPS tag</a> is backup, not a fence. Keep the leash on when guests come and go — doors are the usual exit.</p>\n<p>If the dog is already sound-sensitive, skip the parade and the rooftop. A quiet interior room beats a “he’ll get used to it” experiment.</p>\n<h2>Grills, grease, and people food</h2>\n<p>Grease burns paws and guts. Corn cobs, leftover ribs, and xylitol desserts belong on a high table. Read <a href=\"/learning/foods-toxic-to-dogs\">toxic foods</a>. A stuffed toy in the safe room is cheaper than an ER foreign body.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Walk and toilet before dusk.</li>\n<li>Close curtains and run a fan before the first boom.</li>\n<li>Do not leave citronella candles where they can be knocked over.</li>\n<li>If the dog goes missing, call local shelters the same night and post a clear photo.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Sparklers are not toys for dogs</h2>\n<p>They burn. They get stepped on. Skip the cute photo. Kids with sparklers and a loose dog is a trip to urgent care.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/blog/dog-anxiety.webp\" alt=\"Dog wearing updated ID tags in a quiet interior room with blinds closed on the Fourth of July\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<h2>The morning after</h2>\n<p>Check yards for spent shells the dog could chew. Keep the safe room available for late stragglers. If the dog is still hiding on July 5, call the veterinarian about lingering panic.</p>\n<h2>Shopping a holiday kit</h2>\n<p>ID tags, a secure leash, lick mats. Browse <a href=\"/dog\">the dog aisle</a>.</p>\n<h2>Shop this at Petshiwü</h2>\n<p>Ready to restock after reading about fourth of july dog safety? Petshiwü ships as a nationwide online pet store with free shipping over $49 and no autoship. NYC delivery is available now; nationwide shipping opens soon.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/dog\">Browse matching products at Petshiwü</a> — free shipping over $49, no autoship.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>Should I take my dog to a fireworks show?</h3>\n<p>No. Leave them home in a prepared room.</p>\n<h3>Why do so many dogs go missing on the Fourth?</h3>\n<p>Fear plus open gates plus guests. Prevention is ID and closed doors.</p>\n<h3>Can I crate my dog during fireworks?</h3>\n<p>Only if the crate is already a rest place. A panicked dog can break teeth on bars. See our fireworks and crate guides.</p>\n<h2>Related Petshiwü guides</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/fireworks-anxiety-in-dogs\">Fireworks Anxiety in Dogs: A July Plan That Starts Before Dusk</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/best-gps-dog-tracker-2026\">Best GPS Dog Tracker 2026: Coverage, Battery, and What a Tag Cannot Do</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/foods-toxic-to-dogs\">Foods Toxic to Dogs: The Kitchen List You Should Actually Tape Up</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>This article is educational and is not a diagnosis or treatment plan. Ask your veterinarian before you change food, supplements, or medicine, and seek care right away for blood in stool or vomit, repeated vomiting, trouble breathing, collapse, or a pet that cannot keep water down.</em></p>",
