    "description": "Foods toxic to dogs: xylitol, grapes, onions, chocolate, and the leftovers that send dogs to emergency care. What to do while you call the vet or poison line.",
    "publishedAt": "2026-08-13T14:00:00.000Z",
    "html": "<p>Most kitchen emergencies are boring: gum, raisins, a trash chicken bone. This Petshiwü list is the foods that reliably cause trouble and the first call you make. It is not a complete toxicology textbook. When in doubt, call your veterinarian or a pet poison hotline with the product in your hand.</p>\n<figure>\n  <picture><source srcset=\"/blog/kibble-bowl.webp\" type=\"image/webp\" />\n  <img src=\"/blog/kibble-bowl.jpg\" alt=\"Kitchen counter with labeled no-go foods — xylitol gum, grapes, dark chocolate — kept out of a dog’s reach\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"eager\" /></picture>\n  <figcaption>Kitchen counter with labeled no-go foods — xylitol gum, grapes, dark chocolate — kept out of a dog’s reach</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h2>The usual villains</h2>\n<p>Xylitol (many gums, some peanut butters, “sugar-free” products) can drop blood sugar and damage livers. Grapes and raisins can cause kidney failure in some dogs — there is no known safe amount. Onions and garlic wreck red blood cells. Chocolate, especially dark, is a dose-and-weight problem. Macadamia nuts, raw yeast dough, and high-fat scraps have their own disasters.</p>\n<p>Cooked bones splinter. Corn cobs block. See <a href=\"/learning/homemade-dog-food-safety\">homemade safety</a> before you share dinner.</p>\n<h2>What to do in the first ten minutes</h2>\n<p>Remove access. Estimate how much and when. Call the clinic or a poison hotline before you induce vomiting — some substances burn coming back up, and some dogs should not vomit. Bring the wrapper.</p>\n<p>Do not wait for symptoms if the item is on this list. Waiting is how grape cases present late.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Keep gum and bakeware off coffee tables.</li>\n<li>Close the pantry and the compost.</li>\n<li>Teach guests not to feed the dog.</li>\n<li>If the dog is already collapsing or seizing, go to emergency now.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>People “healthy” foods that are not dog-safe</h2>\n<p>Avocado varies by part and product. Alcohol is a no. Caffeine is a no. Nutmeg in quantity is a no. A tiny accidental crumb is different from a stolen bar of baker’s chocolate — still call.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/blog/kibble-bowl.webp\" alt=\"Kitchen counter with labeled no-go foods — xylitol gum, grapes, dark chocolate — kept out of a dog’s reach\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<h2>Cats have a different list</h2>\n<p>Do not assume the lists match. See <a href=\"/learning/foods-toxic-to-cats\">foods toxic to cats</a>. Lilies are a cat emergency that barely registers for dogs.</p>\n<h2>Shopping prevention, not a mythic antidote</h2>\n<p>Lidded trash, a baby gate, xylitol-free peanut butter if you use it. Browse <a href=\"/dog\">the dog aisle</a> for storage-friendly treats.</p>\n<h2>Shop this at Petshiwü</h2>\n<p>Build the foods toxic to dogs shopping list in our dog collection. Petshiwü offers free shipping over $49, no autoship, NYC delivery now, and nationwide shipping opening soon.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/dog\">Browse matching products at Petshiwü</a> — free shipping over $49, no autoship.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>What if my dog ate a grape?</h3>\n<p>Call your veterinarian or a poison hotline immediately. Do not wait to see if they get lucky.</p>\n<h3>Is dark chocolate worse than milk chocolate?</h3>\n<p>Usually yes, per ounce. The dose still depends on the dog’s weight and how much was eaten. Call anyway.</p>\n<h3>Can I make my dog vomit at home?</h3>\n<p>Only if a veterinarian tells you to and tells you how. Some toxins and some dogs make that dangerous.</p>\n<h2>Related Petshiwü guides</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/foods-toxic-to-cats\">Foods Toxic to Cats: Lilies, Onions, and the Tuna Habit That Backfires</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/homemade-dog-food-safety\">Homemade Dog Food Safety: Why Turkey-and-Rice Is Not a Diet</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/how-to-help-an-overweight-dog\">How to Help an Overweight Dog: Walks, Portions, and Joints That Cannot Wait</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>This article is educational and is not a diagnosis or treatment plan. Ask your veterinarian before you change food, supplements, or medicine, and seek care right away for blood in stool or vomit, repeated vomiting, trouble breathing, collapse, or a pet that cannot keep water down.</em></p>",
