    "title": "Foods Toxic to Cats: Lilies, Onions, and the Tuna Habit That Backfires",
    "description": "Foods toxic to cats: lilies, xylitol, onions, grapes, and why “just a little” people food plus string is an ER story. Call a vet or poison line — do not wait.",
    "publishedAt": "2026-08-15T08:00:00.000Z",
    "html": "<p>Cats investigate with their mouths and then hide the consequences. Lilies can shut down kidneys from pollen on fur. Onions in baby food have wrecked red cells. This Petshiwü list is household plants and leftovers. It is not every toxin on earth. Call a veterinarian or poison hotline with the plant or wrapper in hand.</p>\n<figure>\n  <picture><source srcset=\"/blog/indoor-cat.webp\" type=\"image/webp\" />\n  <img src=\"/blog/indoor-cat.jpg\" alt=\"Bouquet of lilies kept far from a cat, with a note that lilies are a feline emergency\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"eager\" /></picture>\n  <figcaption>Bouquet of lilies kept far from a cat, with a note that lilies are a feline emergency</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h2>Lilies are not a decoration in a cat house</h2>\n<p>True lilies (Lilium and Hemerocallis) are a feline emergency. A nibble or a pollen lick can be enough. If you even think a lily was ingested, go to care — do not wait for vomiting to “confirm.” Choose cat-safe bouquets or skip cut flowers.</p>\n<p>Other plants (sago, some philodendrons) have their own risks. Photograph the plant for the clinic.</p>\n<h2>Kitchen leftovers</h2>\n<p>Onions, garlic, chives — including in broth and jarred sauces. Grapes and raisins. Xylitol in gums and some nut butters. Chocolate. Alcohol. Raw dough. Dog NSAIDs left on a nightstand. A “tiny” onion crumb in leftovers is still a reason to call if a small cat ate a lot of stew.</p>\n<p>String, ribbon, and rubber bands are linear foreign bodies. Tinsel season is predictable. See <a href=\"/learning/picky-cat-not-eating\">appetite changes</a> after a possible string incident.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Do not give dogs’ treats unless they are known cat-safe.</li>\n<li>Skip milk as a treat plan — many cats are lactose intolerant.</li>\n<li>Daily tuna is not a complete diet and can create a tuna-only monster.</li>\n<li>If the cat is already wobbly or not producing urine, that is emergency care.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Essential oils and “natural” cleaners</h2>\n<p>Tea tree and many concentrated oils are dangerous. Diffusers in a closed room with a cat are a common mistake. Ask before you “purify the air.”</p>\n<p><img src=\"/blog/indoor-cat.webp\" alt=\"Bouquet of lilies kept far from a cat, with a note that lilies are a feline emergency\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<h2>Dogs and cats share a kitchen, not a toxin list</h2>\n<p>Grapes worry both. Lilies are the cat-specific headline. Keep both lists taped up — <a href=\"/learning/foods-toxic-to-dogs\">dog toxins</a>.</p>\n<h2>Shopping safer habits</h2>\n<p>Lidded trash, cat-safe plants, treats from <a href=\"/cat\">the cat aisle</a> instead of the table.</p>\n<h2>Shop this at Petshiwü</h2>\n<p>When you are ready to put this foods toxic to cats article into the cart, Petshiwü is a nationwide online pet store with free shipping over $49 and no autoship. NYC delivery is available now; nationwide shipping opens soon.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/cat\">Browse matching products at Petshiwü</a> — free shipping over $49, no autoship.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>My cat bit a lily. What now?</h3>\n<p>This is urgent. Contact a veterinarian or emergency clinic immediately. Do not wait for symptoms.</p>\n<h3>Is chocolate as bad for cats as dogs?</h3>\n<p>It is still toxic. Cats often eat less of it, but you should still call with the amount and type.</p>\n<h3>Can cats have a little onion in broth?</h3>\n<p>No. Onion and garlic are not a seasoning for cats. Use a pet-formulated broth if you use broth at all.</p>\n<h2>Related Petshiwü guides</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/foods-toxic-to-dogs\">Foods Toxic to Dogs: The Kitchen List You Should Actually Tape Up</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/picky-cat-not-eating\">Picky Cat Not Eating: Nausea, Texture, and the 24-Hour Rule</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/best-cat-treats-2026\">Best Cat Treats 2026: Training Bits, Lickables, and the Tiny Calorie Bomb</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>This article is educational and is not a diagnosis or treatment plan. Ask your veterinarian before you change food, supplements, or medicine, and seek care right away for blood in stool or vomit, repeated vomiting, trouble breathing, collapse, or a pet that cannot keep water down.</em></p>",
