    "publishedAt": "2026-08-18T09:00:00.000Z",
    "html": "<p>Bettas are labyrinth fish, not desk toys. A vase or a half-pint unheated bowl is a short, stressful life. This Petshiwü setup is a real tank, a heater, a gentle filter, and a cycle so ammonia does not do the decorating. Pair it with <a href=\"/learning/how-to-cycle-a-fish-tank\">how to cycle a tank</a>.</p>\n<figure>\n  <picture><source srcset=\"/blog/fish-tank.webp\" type=\"image/webp\" />\n  <img src=\"/blog/fish-tank.jpg\" alt=\"Heated, filtered 5-gallon betta tank with silk plants and a thermometer, not a tiny vase\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"eager\" /></picture>\n  <figcaption>Heated, filtered 5-gallon betta tank with silk plants and a thermometer, not a tiny vase</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h2>Give them water they can live in</h2>\n<p>A 5-gallon tank is a common minimum for one male betta in a heated home. Bigger is easier to keep stable. They need tropical temperatures — usually around the mid-70s to low-80s °F — so a heater and a thermometer are not extras. A sponge or gentle filter keeps water moving without turning the fish into a leaf in a storm.</p>\n<p>Condition tap water to remove chlorine. Do not use unfiltered tap as a “fresh start” every day. That resets the good bacteria.</p>\n<h2>Decor that will not shred fins</h2>\n<p>Silk or live plants beat glitter gravel with sharp plastic leaves. A lid helps jumpers. Leave space at the surface — bettas gulp air. Hide spots reduce stress.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cycle the tank before the betta arrives when you can.</li>\n<li>Test ammonia, nitrite, and nitrate — see cycling.</li>\n<li>Do not house two males together.</li>\n<li>Community tankmates are a maybe; many bettas do better alone.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Feeding without a cloudy tank</h2>\n<p>A few pellets once or twice a day, only what they eat in a couple of minutes. Frozen or freeze-dried extras are treats. Overfeeding is the usual cloudy-water story.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/blog/fish-tank.webp\" alt=\"Heated, filtered 5-gallon betta tank with silk plants and a thermometer, not a tiny vase\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<h2>Bowls and “betta kits”</h2>\n<p>If the kit is a cup with a plant, it is a gift box, not a habitat. Skip it. Goldfish have their own size myths — <a href=\"/learning/goldfish-tank-size-truth\">goldfish tank size</a>.</p>\n<h2>Shopping a first betta kit that is actually a kit</h2>\n<p>Tank, heater, gentle filter, conditioner, test kit, silk plants. Browse <a href=\"/fish\">the fish aisle</a>.</p>\n<h2>Shop this at Petshiwü</h2>\n<p>Shop fish supplies at Petshiwü. Free shipping over $49, no autoship. NYC delivery now; nationwide shipping soon.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/fish\">Browse matching products at Petshiwü</a> — free shipping over $49, no autoship.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>Can a betta live in a bowl?</h3>\n<p>A tiny unheated bowl is a poor home. Use a heated, filtered tank with enough water to stay stable.</p>\n<h3>Do bettas need a filter if they have a labyrinth organ?</h3>\n<p>They can gulp air and still need clean water. Filters and water changes handle waste the labyrinth organ does not.</p>\n<h3>How long should I cycle before adding a betta?</h3>\n<p>Until ammonia and nitrite read zero and you understand a water-change schedule. Fish-in cycles are harder on the fish. See our cycling guide.</p>\n<h2>Related Petshiwü guides</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/how-to-cycle-a-fish-tank\">How to Cycle a Fish Tank: Bacteria, Test Kits, and Why “Just Add Fish” Fails</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/goldfish-tank-size-truth\">Goldfish Tank Size Truth: Why a Bowl Is a Myth and Filters Are Not Optional</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/turtle-tank-setup\">Turtle Tank Setup: Water Volume, Basking Dock, and a Filter That Can Keep Up</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>This article is educational and is not a diagnosis or treatment plan. Ask your veterinarian before you change food, supplements, or medicine, and seek care right away for blood in stool or vomit, repeated vomiting, trouble breathing, collapse, or a pet that cannot keep water down.</em></p>",
