    "slug": "how-to-cycle-a-fish-tank",
    "title": "How to Cycle a Fish Tank: Bacteria, Test Kits, and Why “Just Add Fish” Fails",
    "description": "How to cycle a fish tank: ammonia to nitrite to nitrate, how long a cycle takes, fishless versus fish-in, and the water-change habits that keep it stable.",
    "publishedAt": "2026-08-20T10:00:00.000Z",
    "html": "<p>Cycling is growing the bacteria that turn toxic ammonia into less toxic compounds. A pretty tank without that workforce is a poison factory. This Petshiwü walkthrough is the nitrogen story, how to test, and why a bottle of “instant cycle” is a helper at best, not a skip button.</p>\n<figure>\n  <picture><source srcset=\"/blog/fish-tank.webp\" type=\"image/webp\" />\n  <img src=\"/blog/fish-tank.jpg\" alt=\"Aquarium test kit vials beside a new filtered tank during a fishless nitrogen cycle\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"eager\" /></picture>\n  <figcaption>Aquarium test kit vials beside a new filtered tank during a fishless nitrogen cycle</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h2>The three numbers you will live with</h2>\n<p>Fish waste and leftover food become ammonia. Bacteria convert ammonia to nitrite, then other bacteria convert nitrite to nitrate. Ammonia and nitrite should read zero in an established tank. Nitrate is removed with water changes and plants. A liquid test kit beats most strips when you are learning.</p>\n<p>A cycle is done when you can add an ammonia source and see it convert to nitrate with ammonia and nitrite staying at zero. That can take 2–6 weeks, sometimes longer. Heat and a seeded filter sponge from a healthy tank can speed it.</p>\n<h2>Fishless is kinder than fish-in</h2>\n<p>Add a dose of pure ammonia or a bit of fish food and wait, testing every day or two. Do not dump a community of livestock into raw tap water and hope. If you already have fish in an uncycled tank, do frequent water changes to keep ammonia and nitrite down and accept that you are doing the hard version.</p>\n<p>Always condition chlorine out of tap water. Chlorine kills the bacteria you are trying to grow.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Write the test results on a notepad so you see the trend.</li>\n<li>Do not rinse filter media under tap water.</li>\n<li>Do not replace all the water because it looks slightly cloudy — cloudy can be a bacterial bloom.</li>\n<li>Bettas and goldfish still need a cycle — <a href=\"/learning/betta-fish-tank-setup\">betta setup</a>, <a href=\"/learning/goldfish-tank-size-truth\">goldfish size</a>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What bottled bacteria can and cannot do</h2>\n<p>Live nitrifying bacteria products can help if they were stored correctly. They do not replace testing. If the bottle sat hot in a warehouse, assume it is weaker than the label.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/blog/fish-tank.webp\" alt=\"Aquarium test kit vials beside a new filtered tank during a fishless nitrogen cycle\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<h2>After the cycle</h2>\n<p>Add fish slowly. A sudden crowd creates a mini-cycle. Keep a water-change habit based on nitrate and stocking, not a calendar superstition alone.</p>\n<h2>Shopping a first test kit</h2>\n<p>Liquid master test kit, conditioner, a notebook. Browse <a href=\"/fish\">the fish aisle</a>.</p>\n<h2>Shop this at Petshiwü</h2>\n<p>Skip the subscription trap and shop how to cycle a fish tank gear at Petshiwü. Free shipping over $49, no autoship. NYC delivery is available now; nationwide shipping opens soon.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/fish\">Browse matching products at Petshiwü</a> — free shipping over $49, no autoship.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>How do I know the tank is cycled?</h3>\n<p>Ammonia and nitrite stay at zero while nitrate appears after an ammonia challenge. One lucky zero reading is not a cycle.</p>\n<h3>Can I cycle with a betta in the tank?</h3>\n<p>You can, and it is harder on the fish. Prefer fishless. If fish-in, test daily and change water to protect them.</p>\n<h3>Why did my cycle crash after I cleaned?</h3>\n<p>You likely killed the bacteria by washing media in chlorinated water or replacing too much of the system at once.</p>\n<h2>Related Petshiwü guides</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/betta-fish-tank-setup\">Betta Fish Tank Setup: Heat, Filter, and Why a Vase Is Not a Home</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/goldfish-tank-size-truth\">Goldfish Tank Size Truth: Why a Bowl Is a Myth and Filters Are Not Optional</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/turtle-tank-setup\">Turtle Tank Setup: Water Volume, Basking Dock, and a Filter That Can Keep Up</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>This article is educational and is not a diagnosis or treatment plan. Ask your veterinarian before you change food, supplements, or medicine, and seek care right away for blood in stool or vomit, repeated vomiting, trouble breathing, collapse, or a pet that cannot keep water down.</em></p>",
