    "title": "Guinea Pig Diet and Vitamin C: Why They Cannot Make Their Own",
    "description": "Guinea pig diet and vitamin C: hay, pellets, and how to offer vitamin C without sugary drops that go stale. Scurvy signs that mean a vet visit.",
    "publishedAt": "2026-08-25T12:00:00.000Z",
    "html": "<p>Guinea pigs, like people, cannot synthesize vitamin C. A hay-and-pellet diet that forgets C becomes scurvy: sore joints, rough coat, picky eating. This Petshiwü diet is grass hay, a C-stabilized pellet, and fresh veg that actually still has vitamin C in it — not a bottle of drops that oxidized on a sunny shelf.</p>\n<figure>\n  <picture><source srcset=\"/blog/guinea-pig.webp\" type=\"image/webp\" />\n  <img src=\"/blog/guinea-pig.jpg\" alt=\"Guinea pig eating bell pepper and timothy hay next to a vitamin-C pellet bag\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"eager\" /></picture>\n  <figcaption>Guinea pig eating bell pepper and timothy hay next to a vitamin-C pellet bag</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h2>Hay plus a pellet that still has C</h2>\n<p>Unlimited timothy or orchard hay. A guinea-pig-specific pellet (not a rabbit mix) in a measured amount. Vitamin C in pellets degrades with time and heat — buy smaller bags and check dates. Do not use a rabbit pellet and “add a drop.”</p>\n<p>Rabbits do not have the same C requirement. See <a href=\"/learning/what-to-feed-a-rabbit\">rabbit diet</a> if you have both species; feed them as their own animals.</p>\n<h2>Vegetables that do the vitamin job</h2>\n<p>Bell pepper is a reliable C source many pigs enjoy. Leafy greens can rotate. Introduce slowly to avoid diarrhea. Sugary fruit is a treat. Water-bottle vitamin drops often lose potency and taste; food sources are more honest.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Offer C-rich veg daily, not “when you remember.”</li>\n<li>If a pig stops eating, that is urgent — they crash fast.</li>\n<li>Watch for a painful, hunched walk — possible scurvy or dental pain.</li>\n<li>Keep pairs or groups; they are social, but introduce carefully.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>What scurvy can look like</h2>\n<p>Reluctance to move, swollen joints, a rough coat, and bleeding gums. This is a veterinary diagnosis. Do not megadose human tablets without an exotics vet’s math.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/blog/guinea-pig.webp\" alt=\"Guinea pig eating bell pepper and timothy hay next to a vitamin-C pellet bag\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<h2>Housing and chewing</h2>\n<p>They need space to run and hay to wear teeth. A hamster cage is not a pig palace — see <a href=\"/learning/hamster-cage-size-guide\">hamster size</a> for why small cages fail other species too.</p>\n<h2>Shopping a C-aware pantry</h2>\n<p>Fresh hay, dated pig pellets, a pepper habit. Browse <a href=\"/small-animal\">small animals</a>.</p>\n<h2>Shop this at Petshiwü</h2>\n<p>Petshiwü can send the bowls, foods, and extras this guinea pig diet and vitamin c article points to. Free shipping over $49 and no autoship. NYC delivery is available now; nationwide shipping opens soon.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/small-animal\">Browse matching products at Petshiwü</a> — free shipping over $49, no autoship.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>How much vitamin C does a guinea pig need?</h3>\n<p>Needs vary by age and pregnancy. Your exotics veterinarian can confirm a daily target. Food sources plus a proper pellet are the usual path.</p>\n<h3>Can I give orange juice every day?</h3>\n<p>Sugar and acidity make juice a poor daily plan. Use hay, pellets, and vegetables.</p>\n<h3>Why is my guinea pig chewing the cage?</h3>\n<p>Boredom, teeth, or a hay shortage. Fix the diet and space before you buy a louder toy.</p>\n<h2>Related Petshiwü guides</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/what-to-feed-a-rabbit\">What to Feed a Rabbit: Hay First, Pellets Second, and the Treat Trap</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/chinchilla-dust-bath\">Chinchilla Dust Bath: How Often, Which Dust, and Why Water Baths Are a No</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/hamster-cage-size-guide\">Hamster Cage Size Guide: Floor Space, Wheels, and Why a Critter Tank Beats a Tiny Castle</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>This article is educational and is not a diagnosis or treatment plan. Ask your veterinarian before you change food, supplements, or medicine, and seek care right away for blood in stool or vomit, repeated vomiting, trouble breathing, collapse, or a pet that cannot keep water down.</em></p>",
