    "slug": "bird-enrichment-ideas",
    "title": "Bird Enrichment Ideas: Foraging, Flight Time, and Toys That Are Not Mirrors Only",
    "description": "Bird enrichment ideas: foraging wraps, rotation schedules, supervised out-of-cage time, and why a single bell is not a life. Species-safe materials only.",
    "publishedAt": "2026-09-01T08:00:00.000Z",
    "html": "<p>Birds are not decorations. A bored parrot screams, plucks, or both. Enrichment is foraging, chewing, bathing, and social time — rotated so Tuesday is not identical to November. This Petshiwü idea list is a starting menu. An avian veterinarian should see a bird that suddenly stops talking, fluffs all day, or cannot perch.</p>\n<figure>\n  <picture><source srcset=\"/blog/bird.webp\" type=\"image/webp\" />\n  <img src=\"/blog/bird.jpg\" alt=\"Parrot shredding a foraging toy on a play stand away from the kitchen, showing enrichment outside the cage\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"eager\" /></picture>\n  <figcaption>Parrot shredding a foraging toy on a play stand away from the kitchen, showing enrichment outside the cage</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h2>Foraging is the main job</h2>\n<p>Wrap pellets in paper, hide them in a cardboard cup, or use a foraging toy that makes the bird work. Seed-only bowls are a snack bar that ends in fatty liver. See <a href=\"/learning/parakeet-diet-guide\">parakeet diet</a> for the bowl half of the day.</p>\n<p>Rotate toys weekly. A toy that has been ignored for a month can become new again after a rest in the closet.</p>\n<h2>Out-of-cage time that is actually safe</h2>\n<p>Closed toilets, no Teflon fumes, no open pots of water, no ceiling fans. Windows closed. Birds fly into glass. Supervised play stands beat a free-roam kitchen during dinner. Dogs and birds need a plan — curiosity is not cute when it is a grab.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Offer a shallow bath or a mist if the species enjoys it.</li>\n<li>Chew toys should be bird-safe wood and leather, not zinc-heavy jewelry.</li>\n<li>Mirrors can become a social obsession for some birds — limit if behavior goes odd.</li>\n<li>If screaming is new and constant, think medical plus boredom, not “spite.”</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Training is enrichment</h2>\n<p>Target training and a step-up cue give the bird a conversation with you that is not “stop screaming.” Keep sessions short. Pay with a reserved favorite food, not the whole diet.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/blog/bird.webp\" alt=\"Parrot shredding a foraging toy on a play stand away from the kitchen, showing enrichment outside the cage\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<h2>Species differences</h2>\n<p>A budgie, a cockatiel, and a macaw do not share a toy size. What is a foot toy for one is a swallow for another. When unsure, go larger and simpler.</p>\n<h2>Shopping a rotation box</h2>\n<p>Foraging toys, shreddables, a play stand. Browse <a href=\"/bird\">birds</a>.</p>\n<h2>Shop this at Petshiwü</h2>\n<p>Shop bird supplies at Petshiwü. Free shipping over $49, no autoship. NYC delivery now; nationwide shipping soon.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/bird\">Browse matching products at Petshiwü</a> — free shipping over $49, no autoship.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>How many hours out of the cage does a parrot need?</h3>\n<p>Many companion parrots do better with several supervised hours, but the exact number depends on species and your home’s safety. The cage still has to be a rich place when they are inside.</p>\n<h3>Why is my bird screaming at dawn?</h3>\n<p>Flock-call timing is normal-ish. A sudden change in voice or energy is a vet question. Enrichment helps the rest of the day.</p>\n<h3>Are essential-oil diffusers OK near birds?</h3>\n<p>Generally a bad idea. Bird lungs are sensitive. Skip the spa mist.</p>\n<h2>Related Petshiwü guides</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/parakeet-diet-guide\">Parakeet Diet Guide: Pellets, Seed, and the Chop That Is Not a Suggestion</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/flying-with-a-cat\">Flying with a Cat: Carrier Training, Airline Rules, and Sedation Myths</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/best-puzzle-toys-for-dogs\">Best Puzzle Toys for Dogs: Difficulty Levels That Do Not Create a Genius Criminal</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>This article is educational and is not a diagnosis or treatment plan. Ask your veterinarian before you change food, supplements, or medicine, and seek care right away for blood in stool or vomit, repeated vomiting, trouble breathing, collapse, or a pet that cannot keep water down.</em></p>",
    "featuredImage": "/blog/bird.jpg",
    "featuredImageWebp": "/blog/bird.webp",
