    "slug": "hedgehog-care-for-beginners",
    "title": "Hedgehog Care for Beginners: Heat, Wheels, and Hands-Off Nights",
    "description": "Hedgehog care for beginners: cage warmth, a solid wheel, insect-and-kibble feeding, and why a cold, rolled-up hedgehog is a veterinary emergency.",
    "publishedAt": "2026-09-06T10:00:00.000Z",
    "html": "<p>African pygmy hedgehogs sold as pets are nocturnal insectivores who need steady warmth. A cold house can push them toward a dangerous hibernation attempt. This Petshiwü beginner guide is heat, a safe wheel, and handling that does not turn every pickup into a ball of grump. An exotics vet should be on file before you need one.</p>\n<figure>\n  <picture><source srcset=\"/blog/hamster.webp\" type=\"image/webp\" />\n  <img src=\"/blog/hamster.jpg\" alt=\"Pet hedgehog on a solid-surface exercise wheel in a warm enclosure with a hide box\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"eager\" /></picture>\n  <figcaption>Pet hedgehog on a solid-surface exercise wheel in a warm enclosure with a hide box</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h2>Temperature is not optional</h2>\n<p>Most keepers aim for a warm room in the mid-70s °F and watch for too-hot as well as too-cold. A ceramic heat emitter or a carefully placed heat source with a thermostat beats a heating pad the hedgehog can burn on. If they are cool to the touch, wobbly, or stuck rolled up, warm them gently and call the vet — do not put them on a blasting space heater.</p>\n<p>They are solitary. One hedgehog per enclosure unless a specialist said otherwise.</p>\n<h2>Wheels and flooring</h2>\n<p>A solid wheel (no wire rungs) lets them run without a broken leg. Deep, dusty bedding can irritate. Provide a hide. Cage size should let them trot, not just turn around — the same spirit as <a href=\"/learning/hamster-cage-size-guide\">hamster space</a>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Offer a shallow dish of water they cannot drown in.</li>\n<li>Diet is typically a high-quality hedgehog or specified cat kibble plus insects your vet agrees with — not a bowl of grapes.</li>\n<li>Handle low to the ground so a fall is short.</li>\n<li>Salmonellosis is a real hygiene topic: wash hands, keep them out of the kitchen sink.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Quilling and attitude</h2>\n<p>Young hogs quill and can be extra prickly. That is not a reason to squeeze. Let them explore your lap with a towel. If they never unball and lose weight, book the vet.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/blog/hamster.webp\" alt=\"Pet hedgehog on a solid-surface exercise wheel in a warm enclosure with a hide box\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<h2>Legal and vet access</h2>\n<p>Some places restrict hedgehog ownership. Confirm the law. Confirm you have an exotics clinic before you buy the animal.</p>\n<h2>Shopping a warm, simple setup</h2>\n<p>Thermostat heat, solid wheel, hide, appropriate food. Browse <a href=\"/small-animal\">small animals</a>.</p>\n<h2>Shop this at Petshiwü</h2>\n<p>Skip the subscription trap and shop hedgehog care for beginners gear at Petshiwü. Free shipping over $49, no autoship. NYC delivery is available now; nationwide shipping opens soon.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/small-animal\">Browse matching products at Petshiwü</a> — free shipping over $49, no autoship.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>Why is my hedgehog not coming out?</h3>\n<p>They are nocturnal. Daytime hiding can be normal. Daytime lethargy plus cold is not. Check temperature and call a vet if you are unsure.</p>\n<h3>Can hedgehogs eat mealworms only?</h3>\n<p>Insects are part of a diet, not the whole diet, unless an exotics veterinarian designed it. Fatty worm-only menus create problems.</p>\n<h3>Do they need a friend?</h3>\n<p>Usually no. They can fight. Enrichment and correct heat beat a forced roommate.</p>\n<h2>Related Petshiwü guides</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/ferret-diet-guide\">Ferret Diet Guide: Obligate Carnivores, Not a Hamster Mix</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/hamster-cage-size-guide\">Hamster Cage Size Guide: Floor Space, Wheels, and Why a Critter Tank Beats a Tiny Castle</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/crested-gecko-care\">Crested Gecko Care: Humidity, Heights, and a Diet That Is Not Just Crickets</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>This article is educational and is not a diagnosis or treatment plan. Ask your veterinarian before you change food, supplements, or medicine, and seek care right away for blood in stool or vomit, repeated vomiting, trouble breathing, collapse, or a pet that cannot keep water down.</em></p>",
