    "description": "Crested gecko care: vertical enclosure, humidity swings, commercial gecko diet, and why a dry screen cage in Arizona is a different problem than a swamp box.",
    "publishedAt": "2026-09-11T12:00:00.000Z",
    "html": "<p>Crested geckos are nocturnal climbers from humid forests. They need height, hides, and a humidity cycle that lets them shed. They do not need a desert basking rock. This Petshiwü care sheet is enclosure and diet. A gecko that drops tail weight or has stuck shed around toes needs an exotics veterinarian.</p>\n<figure>\n  <picture><source srcset=\"/blog/reptile.webp\" type=\"image/webp\" />\n  <img src=\"/blog/reptile.jpg\" alt=\"Crested gecko on a vertical branch in a planted humid enclosure with a diet cup on the wall\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"eager\" /></picture>\n  <figcaption>Crested gecko on a vertical branch in a planted humid enclosure with a diet cup on the wall</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h2>Go vertical</h2>\n<p>A tall enclosure with branches, leaves, and at least one moist hide beats a long low tank. Screen cages dry out in arid homes; glass holds humidity but can grow mold if you never air it. Provide a day/night light cycle. They do not use UVB the way a bearded dragon does, though some keepers offer low-level UV — ask your vet. See <a href=\"/learning/bearded-dragon-lighting-guide\">beardie lighting</a> for the opposite lighting philosophy.</p>\n<p>Temps are moderate. Overheating is easy on a sunny windowsill.</p>\n<h2>Diet is mostly a complete powder</h2>\n<p>A commercial crested gecko diet mixed to the right consistency is the usual staple. Insects can be an extra if your vet agrees, not a random cricket free-for-all. Fruit baby food as the only diet is a deficiency plan. Remove uneaten diet before it spoils.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mist to create a humidity spike, then let it drop — a constant swamp invites mold.</li>\n<li>Watch sheds; stuck eye caps and toe rings are emergencies.</li>\n<li>Handle low and less if the gecko is new — they jump.</li>\n<li>If they stop licking diet and lose weight, book the vet.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Supplements and calcium</h2>\n<p>Follow the diet maker and your veterinarian. Do not invent a dusting schedule from three forums.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/blog/reptile.webp\" alt=\"Crested gecko on a vertical branch in a planted humid enclosure with a diet cup on the wall\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<h2>Cohabitation</h2>\n<p>Adults can fight. Housing together is a specialist decision, not a default. One gecko, one enclosure is the beginner path.</p>\n<h2>Shopping a tall, humid setup</h2>\n<p>Vertical tank, complete diet, hygrometer. Browse <a href=\"/reptile\">reptiles</a>.</p>\n<h2>Shop this at Petshiwü</h2>\n<p>Petshiwü can send the bowls, foods, and extras this crested gecko care article points to. Free shipping over $49 and no autoship. NYC delivery is available now; nationwide shipping opens soon.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/reptile\">Browse matching products at Petshiwü</a> — free shipping over $49, no autoship.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>How often do I mist a crested gecko?</h3>\n<p>Often nightly or as needed to spike humidity, then allow a dry-out. Use a hygrometer. Constant dripping wet is not the goal.</p>\n<h3>Do they need a heat lamp?</h3>\n<p>They need a safe ambient range, not a dragon basking lamp. Overheating kills. Measure, do not guess.</p>\n<h3>Why did my gecko drop its tail?</h3>\n<p>Stress, a grab, or a scare. The tail does not grow back like some lizards. Reduce handling and check husbandry.</p>\n<h2>Related Petshiwü guides</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/bearded-dragon-lighting-guide\">Bearded Dragon Lighting Guide: UVB, Heat, and the Gradient That Makes Vitamin D</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/chinchilla-dust-bath\">Chinchilla Dust Bath: How Often, Which Dust, and Why Water Baths Are a No</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/hedgehog-care-for-beginners\">Hedgehog Care for Beginners: Heat, Wheels, and Hands-Off Nights</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>This article is educational and is not a diagnosis or treatment plan. Ask your veterinarian before you change food, supplements, or medicine, and seek care right away for blood in stool or vomit, repeated vomiting, trouble breathing, collapse, or a pet that cannot keep water down.</em></p>",
