    "slug": "chinchilla-dust-bath",
    "title": "Chinchilla Dust Bath: How Often, Which Dust, and Why Water Baths Are a No",
    "description": "Chinchilla dust bath: how many times a week, volcanic dust versus sand, bath-house setup, and why a wet bath can chill and mat a chinchilla’s coat.",
    "publishedAt": "2026-09-13T13:00:00.000Z",
    "html": "<p>Chinchillas clean with dust, not shampoo. Their dense fur holds moisture and can grow fungus if you invent a spa day. This Petshiwü bath guide is a dust house, a timer, and the humidity habits that keep the coat from matting. A chin that stops eating or sits fluffed in a corner needs an exotics veterinarian.</p>\n<figure>\n  <picture><source srcset=\"/blog/hamster.webp\" type=\"image/webp\" />\n  <img src=\"/blog/hamster.jpg\" alt=\"Chinchilla rolling in a dust-bath house filled with fine chinchilla dust, not water\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"eager\" /></picture>\n  <figcaption>Chinchilla rolling in a dust-bath house filled with fine chinchilla dust, not water</figcaption>\n</figure>\n<h2>Dust, not playground sand</h2>\n<p>Use dust sold for chinchillas — often a fine volcanic or adakitic dust. Reptile calcium sand and children’s play sand are the wrong particle and the wrong chemistry. Pour a few inches into a sturdy bath house they can roll in without tipping. After they finish, remove the house so they do not live in a dust bowl 24/7.</p>\n<p>Many keepers offer a bath several times a week, not constantly. If the fur looks greasy, you may need a bath sooner. If the eyes look irritated, the dust is too dusty or too frequent — ask your vet.</p>\n<h2>Never a water bath unless a vet said so</h2>\n<p>Water soaks the coat, chills them, and is hard to dry. They are Andes animals who prefer cool, dry rooms. High household humidity is a coat problem. A damp basement is a poor chin room.</p>\n<p>Diet is hay-heavy like other herbivores — see <a href=\"/learning/what-to-feed-a-rabbit\">rabbit hay logic</a> for the spirit, but use chinchilla-appropriate pellets and an exotics-vetted list. Guinea pigs still need extra C — <a href=\"/learning/guinea-pig-diet-and-vitamin-c\">pig diet</a>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Keep the cage out of direct sun and away from radiators.</li>\n<li>Provide a hide and a safe wheel if your vet likes the model.</li>\n<li>They chew everything — no mystery wood or electrical cords.</li>\n<li>If you see fur slip or bald patches, book the vet; do not just add more dust.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Cleaning the dust</h2>\n<p>Sift out poops. Replace dust when it looks dirty. A covered bath house keeps the room from looking like a volcano.</p>\n<p><img src=\"/blog/hamster.webp\" alt=\"Chinchilla rolling in a dust-bath house filled with fine chinchilla dust, not water\" width=\"1200\" height=\"675\" loading=\"lazy\" /></p>\n<h2>Handling after a bath</h2>\n<p>Let them finish the roll. Do not immediately squeeze a dusty chin into your sweater if you have asthma — they fog the air. Wash your hands.</p>\n<h2>Shopping a bath kit</h2>\n<p>Chinchilla dust, a bath house, hay. Browse <a href=\"/small-animal\">small animals</a>.</p>\n<h2>Shop this at Petshiwü</h2>\n<p>Ready to restock after reading about chinchilla dust bath? Petshiwü ships as a nationwide online pet store with free shipping over $49 and no autoship. NYC delivery is available now; nationwide shipping opens soon.</p>\n<p><a href=\"/small-animal\">Browse matching products at Petshiwü</a> — free shipping over $49, no autoship.</p>\n<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>\n<h3>How often should a chinchilla take a dust bath?</h3>\n<p>Commonly several times a week for 10–15 minutes, then remove the dust. Adjust if the coat looks oily or the eyes look irritated.</p>\n<h3>Can I use sand instead of dust?</h3>\n<p>Play sand is too coarse and dusty in the wrong way. Use chinchilla-specific dust.</p>\n<h3>Why is my chinchilla chewing the cage?</h3>\n<p>Boredom, teeth, or a diet short on hay. Fix space and chew options. Dental pain needs a vet.</p>\n<h2>Related Petshiwü guides</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/what-to-feed-a-rabbit\">What to Feed a Rabbit: Hay First, Pellets Second, and the Treat Trap</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/guinea-pig-diet-and-vitamin-c\">Guinea Pig Diet and Vitamin C: Why They Cannot Make Their Own</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/learning/crested-gecko-care\">Crested Gecko Care: Humidity, Heights, and a Diet That Is Not Just Crickets</a></li>\n</ul>\n<p><em>This article is educational and is not a diagnosis or treatment plan. Ask your veterinarian before you change food, supplements, or medicine, and seek care right away for blood in stool or vomit, repeated vomiting, trouble breathing, collapse, or a pet that cannot keep water down.</em></p>",
    "featuredImage": "/blog/hamster.jpg",
    "featuredImageWebp": "/blog/hamster.webp",
